WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club


00:00:06.925 --> 00:00:08.800
For me, he was like a friend
that I could go to.


00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:11.134
This is the Olympic doctor.


00:00:11.217 --> 00:00:13.134
I was brainwashed to believe


00:00:13.217 --> 00:00:15.135
this was a valid
medical procedure.


00:00:16.135 --> 00:00:18.844
Wow! What have you done?


00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:21.469
So many people came forward.


00:00:21.802 --> 00:00:22.885
They're taking back their voice


00:00:22.969 --> 00:00:24.219
but they shouldn't have had
to use it.


00:00:24.553 --> 00:00:26.302
Remember what you have witnessed...


00:00:28.345 --> 00:00:30.970
because everything is
what these survivors are worth.


00:01:26.059 --> 00:01:27.934
Child protection hotline.
This is Candice speaking.


00:01:28.017 --> 00:01:29.185
How can I help you?


00:01:29.269 --> 00:01:31.310
Child protection hotline.
This is Crystal.


00:01:31.394 --> 00:01:33.477
Hi. This is Steve Reid. Child protection hotline.
How may I help you?


00:01:33.561 --> 00:01:35.061
And what is the youth's ethnicity
and primary language?


00:01:35.145 --> 00:01:36.478
Yes, officer. Uh-huh.


00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:38.145
And what is the name
of the minor?


00:01:38.228 --> 00:01:39.894
Does the child have
any physical disability?


00:01:39.978 --> 00:01:42.061
DCFS. Okay.
Can I have the address there?


00:01:42.145 --> 00:01:43.311
What is the home address?


00:01:45.978 --> 00:01:48.145
So, child number one.
What's child number one's name?


00:01:48.228 --> 00:01:49.479
Okay.


00:01:49.563 --> 00:01:50.979
Have you guys
contacted law enforcement?


00:01:51.062 --> 00:01:52.480
So, basically, seven years old.


00:01:52.564 --> 00:01:54.021
Any issues of domestic violence


00:01:54.105 --> 00:01:55.480
-between mother and father?
-And now


00:01:55.564 --> 00:01:56.480
you're calling
about child number two.


00:01:56.564 --> 00:01:57.855
No injuries.


00:01:57.938 --> 00:01:59.313
And you're
a school psychologist,


00:01:59.397 --> 00:02:00.605
and what school
are you calling from?


00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:02.565
Any substance abuse?


00:02:02.648 --> 00:02:04.606
Have you
contacted her social worker?


00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:06.565
Are there any other children
in the home?


00:02:06.648 --> 00:02:08.481
Any other children in the home?


00:02:08.565 --> 00:02:09.814
Any siblings?


00:02:11.648 --> 00:02:14.772
Okay?


00:02:19.191 --> 00:02:20.941
Approximately how old?


00:02:22.066 --> 00:02:24.108
What's the allegation here?


00:02:25.275 --> 00:02:26.525
Mm-hmm.


00:02:26.608 --> 00:02:28.024
Okay, so, we--


00:02:28.108 --> 00:02:30.775
Ma'am, you need to slow down
a little bit. Um...


00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:32.693
Okay,
and what were the concerns


00:02:32.775 --> 00:02:34.109
that were expressed to you?


00:02:59.654 --> 00:03:03.280
Denyshia? Get up, baby.


00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:06.655
Good morning.


00:03:11.905 --> 00:03:14.072
Sydney, you wanna get up
and start making your bed, baby?


00:03:14.156 --> 00:03:16.114
Get up. Denyshia will be out
in a minute.


00:03:16.197 --> 00:03:17.281
Okay.


00:03:20.656 --> 00:03:23.197
Adrienne? Adrienne?


00:03:23.282 --> 00:03:24.699
Come on, baby, time to get up.


00:03:27.241 --> 00:03:28.533
I'm tired.


00:03:28.616 --> 00:03:31.158
I know you guys are tired,
but you gotta get up.


00:03:34.782 --> 00:03:37.701
Casi? Come on, baby.


00:03:42.867 --> 00:03:44.200
Sleeping good.


00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:49.660
Oh, you're being silly.


00:03:51.369 --> 00:03:52.910
I've been a foster parent


00:03:52.994 --> 00:03:55.369
for at least 27 years.


00:03:55.453 --> 00:03:58.369
I've had all kinda kids
that come in and outta my home,


00:03:58.453 --> 00:04:01.119
but I try to conquer them all.


00:04:01.202 --> 00:04:03.035
Makes me feel good
when I get a child


00:04:03.119 --> 00:04:05.287
that I have really helped.


00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.245
I don't want a child
that I'm not doing nothing for.


00:04:08.328 --> 00:04:11.537
They're just here eating and
sleeping and going to school.


00:04:11.621 --> 00:04:13.204
I like challenge,


00:04:13.288 --> 00:04:15.871
and I think I mighta asked for
too much challenge...


00:04:15.954 --> 00:04:18.997
...'cause sometime
I really get it. But, uh...


00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.038
so far, so good.


00:04:23.747 --> 00:04:25.872
Okay, it's time
for our morning prayer.


00:04:27.497 --> 00:04:29.038
Okay, you guys grab hands.


00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.040
You wanna pray first, Sydney?


00:04:35.124 --> 00:04:37.332
Yeah. God, protect us every day.


00:04:37.416 --> 00:04:40.040
Keep us safe.
Amen.


00:04:40.124 --> 00:04:42.832
-What a-- a quick prayer.


00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.416
Denyshia, do you wanna do it?


00:04:46.041 --> 00:04:49.750
Casi, you wanna pray?
Okay, go on.


00:04:59.752 --> 00:05:02.752
Very good. Very good, Casi.


00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:05.711
Only God knows what you're
saying, but that's very good.


00:05:05.793 --> 00:05:07.335
Lord, we ask you
to bless these kids


00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:09.586
as they leave this morning
going to school.


00:05:09.669 --> 00:05:10.960
Ask you protect them, Lord,


00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:12.670
and keep them safe
in their classroom,


00:05:12.753 --> 00:05:14.003
on the playground.


00:05:14.086 --> 00:05:16.712
Let them be respectful
to their teacher.


00:05:16.794 --> 00:05:19.336
Bless your knowledge, Lord.
Let them be, uh,


00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.629
alert in what
they're doing in class.


00:05:21.713 --> 00:05:22.671
-Amen.
-Amen.


00:05:22.754 --> 00:05:23.962
All right.


00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:29.255
I was about eight
when I got in foster care.


00:05:30.505 --> 00:05:32.714
My parents were doing drugs
and stuff.


00:05:32.796 --> 00:05:37.422
Like, they were just drinking,
drugs, being bad.


00:05:37.505 --> 00:05:39.298
I was abandoned,


00:05:39.381 --> 00:05:41.256
and I was, um,


00:05:41.339 --> 00:05:43.548
abused, and I was mistreated,


00:05:43.631 --> 00:05:46.340
and, like, no one showed
that they cared for me.


00:05:47.923 --> 00:05:51.048
I've been here
going on five years.


00:05:51.132 --> 00:05:55.300
At first, I was very depressed.


00:05:55.383 --> 00:05:58.425
I didn't think, like,
no one would ever want me,


00:05:58.508 --> 00:06:02.592
and that, like,
something was wrong with me,


00:06:02.675 --> 00:06:04.175
and, uh...


00:06:06.676 --> 00:06:09.385
I didn't, like--
I don't know-- I didn't, like...


00:06:11.177 --> 00:06:13.801
I didn't know that I could
actually be loved again,


00:06:13.885 --> 00:06:15.051
or ever.


00:06:42.804 --> 00:06:44.597
So, what do you have upcoming?


00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:46.681
This is my 17-year-old.


00:06:46.764 --> 00:06:48.181
Almost gonna be 18.


00:06:48.264 --> 00:06:51.181
Yes, just graduated high school.


00:06:51.264 --> 00:06:53.473
This is a kid
who doesn't have parents,


00:06:53.556 --> 00:06:55.140
doesn't have mentors,


00:06:55.223 --> 00:06:57.265
who doesn't have anyone who's
been consistent in his life.


00:06:57.348 --> 00:06:59.348
You've had him for a while now.
What, two years?


00:06:59.432 --> 00:07:02.849
Yep. To see him trust me
is a big deal to me.


00:07:02.932 --> 00:07:06.016
Sometimes I feel like I'm
so close in age to my kids


00:07:06.099 --> 00:07:08.932
that they're, like, not--
they're not gonna respect me,


00:07:09.016 --> 00:07:11.308
or they're not gonna be able
to take information from me,


00:07:11.391 --> 00:07:13.183
but in a lotta ways, I feel like


00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:15.642
it made me more trustworthy
to them,


00:07:15.726 --> 00:07:17.976
and so when I gave him advice,
he actually took it.


00:07:20.934 --> 00:07:23.185
Well, I never wanted
to be a social worker.


00:07:23.268 --> 00:07:25.393
That's for sure.


00:07:25.477 --> 00:07:29.352
A lot of my youth was spent
in the child welfare system.


00:07:29.436 --> 00:07:33.603
I hated the system,
and I hated social workers.


00:07:35.394 --> 00:07:37.728
I had a lot of negative things
to say about DCFS,


00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.144
and everyone would say,


00:07:39.227 --> 00:07:40.895
"You need to walk
in their shoes.


00:07:40.978 --> 00:07:42.812
You don't know what it's like
on the other side."


00:07:42.896 --> 00:07:44.521
And to really be
a true advocate,


00:07:44.605 --> 00:07:45.896
you need to know


00:07:45.979 --> 00:07:47.480
where the other side
is coming from.


00:07:49.021 --> 00:07:51.937
When I started this job,
it was very humbling


00:07:52.021 --> 00:07:53.730
because, like,
you don't know anything.


00:07:53.812 --> 00:07:56.647
You don't know anything but
what they choose to tell you,


00:07:56.731 --> 00:07:58.272
and, hopefully,
some of that is the truth,


00:07:58.355 --> 00:08:01.230
so you can try to provide
some services to the family.


00:08:01.314 --> 00:08:03.147
This job is hard as hell.


00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:18.399
This is Larry Petrosky.


00:08:18.483 --> 00:08:19.566
Reporting abuse?


00:08:19.649 --> 00:08:21.107
Yes, sir.


00:08:21.192 --> 00:08:22.775
-You a mandated reporter?
-I am.


00:08:22.858 --> 00:08:24.400
And who abused the child


00:08:24.484 --> 00:08:27.192
It's a substance-exposed infant.


00:08:27.275 --> 00:08:29.859
Mother tested positive
for what?


00:08:29.942 --> 00:08:31.359
Cocaine.


00:08:31.443 --> 00:08:32.942
What about the baby?


00:08:33.026 --> 00:08:34.860
The infant's urine is negative,


00:08:34.943 --> 00:08:38.736
but we missed the first one,
and the meconium is pending.


00:08:38.818 --> 00:08:40.736
Baby still at the hospital?


00:08:40.818 --> 00:08:41.943
Yes, sir.


00:08:42.027 --> 00:08:43.652
And when gonna be discharged?


00:08:43.736 --> 00:08:46.027
They will both
be ready for discharge 12/04.


00:08:46.818 --> 00:08:48.570
That's tomorrow.


00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:53.196
When I get a referral
from the hotline,


00:08:53.279 --> 00:08:56.404
I will get basic information
about the family,


00:08:56.488 --> 00:09:00.904
what the safety concern is,
what the allegations are.


00:09:00.987 --> 00:09:03.863
It's considered
an immediate response referral


00:09:03.946 --> 00:09:07.155
because of the discharge date
for the child.


00:09:07.238 --> 00:09:09.614
We don't wanna send
a newborn home


00:09:09.697 --> 00:09:14.197
with a mom who potentially
has this cocaine addiction.


00:09:19.324 --> 00:09:21.491
I always had
a passion for children


00:09:21.574 --> 00:09:24.324
and being a voice
for the voiceless.


00:09:24.407 --> 00:09:27.990
That's why I became
a social worker.


00:09:28.073 --> 00:09:31.158
I always compare
being a DCFS social worker


00:09:31.241 --> 00:09:32.824
like being in the Marines,


00:09:32.908 --> 00:09:35.492
'cause we're out in the field,
in the front lines.


00:09:40.117 --> 00:09:42.326
The social worker came in.


00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:43.660
Chris is asking, like,


00:09:43.785 --> 00:09:45.702
"Why is there
a social worker here?"


00:09:45.785 --> 00:09:47.368
The social worker had told me


00:09:47.452 --> 00:09:49.785
that I tested positive
for cocaine,


00:09:50.993 --> 00:09:52.702
and that when my daughter,


00:09:52.785 --> 00:09:54.619
you know,
when my daughter comes,


00:09:54.702 --> 00:09:57.161
that they're gonna test her.


00:09:57.244 --> 00:09:58.911
And so...


00:09:58.994 --> 00:10:00.994
I-- the first question
I asked is,


00:10:01.078 --> 00:10:02.870
"Are they gonna take her
from me?"


00:10:04.162 --> 00:10:05.953
There's baby Kris'Lyn.


00:10:10.705 --> 00:10:12.497
And then the next day,


00:10:12.580 --> 00:10:14.996
that's when
the emergency worker came,


00:10:15.079 --> 00:10:16.497
Jacqueline,


00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:20.205
and that's when she
started asking us questions.


00:10:20.288 --> 00:10:22.413
I came up with a story.


00:10:22.497 --> 00:10:25.998
It wasn't a lie.
We did go to a friend's house,


00:10:26.081 --> 00:10:28.415
and I told her
that there was drugs there.


00:10:28.499 --> 00:10:30.790
They were smoking the cocaine.


00:10:30.873 --> 00:10:34.415
And I told her just
this big old lie.


00:10:34.499 --> 00:10:37.999
Chris was scared,
so I told him


00:10:38.082 --> 00:10:43.500
to just go along with it,
because he didn't know I use.


00:10:43.583 --> 00:10:46.583
I talked to mom,
and then I talked to dad,


00:10:46.666 --> 00:10:49.917
and they gave me
the same story, that...


00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.918
she tested positive for cocaine
because of secondhand smoke.


00:10:54.001 --> 00:10:56.210
So, after talking
to the medical professionals,


00:10:56.293 --> 00:10:57.876
they tell me
that's not possible.


00:10:57.959 --> 00:11:00.460
And so, at that point, we have
concerns for mom's drug use,


00:11:00.543 --> 00:11:02.335
and we also have concerns
that dad's


00:11:02.418 --> 00:11:04.294
not gonna be protective
of the baby


00:11:04.377 --> 00:11:07.503
and because it seems like
he's covering for her.


00:11:09.544 --> 00:11:12.670
I felt betrayed, lied to.


00:11:13.961 --> 00:11:16.378
I left. Went for a walk.


00:11:17.546 --> 00:11:19.630
I was just thinking.


00:11:19.713 --> 00:11:21.213
She's been here with me,


00:11:21.296 --> 00:11:24.296
dealing with me,
for five years.


00:11:24.379 --> 00:11:29.213
This is her first time she ever
really, really messed up.


00:11:30.714 --> 00:11:33.297
I took dad outside,
and says, like,


00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:36.131
"Look, we're going for
a removal order,


00:11:36.215 --> 00:11:38.048
so you need to be
honest with me.


00:11:38.131 --> 00:11:40.131
Does she have an addiction?
Is she using?"


00:11:40.215 --> 00:11:41.757
And he continued to say,


00:11:41.839 --> 00:11:45.007
"No, no, no.
I don't know anything."


00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.549
We have this short amount
of time to make this decision


00:11:47.633 --> 00:11:50.799
whether we can release
the baby to the father,


00:11:50.882 --> 00:11:52.132
and at that time,


00:11:52.216 --> 00:11:53.591
with the information
that we had,


00:11:53.674 --> 00:11:56.090
we couldn't ensure
the safety of the child


00:11:56.174 --> 00:11:57.424
in the care of the father.


00:11:57.508 --> 00:12:00.510
So we moved forward
with the removal order.


00:12:17.344 --> 00:12:20.511
I was born a drug baby.


00:12:20.594 --> 00:12:23.803
My mom was on cocaine,
crystal meth.


00:12:25.221 --> 00:12:26.845
I got taken away right away


00:12:26.929 --> 00:12:29.095
and put into
the foster care system.


00:12:30.471 --> 00:12:34.221
I went from home to home
to home to home,


00:12:34.304 --> 00:12:36.679
sometimes with my sister,
sometimes not.


00:12:36.763 --> 00:12:39.805
Overall, I have been in about...


00:12:42.805 --> 00:12:46.389
I don't know, 16, 17, 18 homes.


00:12:46.473 --> 00:12:49.139
I've always called every single
one of my foster parents


00:12:49.223 --> 00:12:50.515
Mom and Dad,


00:12:50.598 --> 00:12:52.057
but, like, I always knew that


00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:55.641
my real mom
was the lady that we'd visit.


00:13:00.098 --> 00:13:01.641
Hey, Mary.


00:13:01.724 --> 00:13:03.682
-Jennifer. Nice to meet you.
-Hi. Nice to meet you. I'm Mary.


00:13:03.766 --> 00:13:05.600
I'm your attorney.
This is Sondra.


00:13:05.683 --> 00:13:07.225
-Hi. Nice to meet you.
-Hi, Sondra.


00:13:07.308 --> 00:13:08.392
How you holdin' up?


00:13:08.476 --> 00:13:10.601
Um, I'm okay.


00:13:10.684 --> 00:13:12.726
So, what's kinda different
about what we do now,


00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.518
versus when you were younger,
is you're in a new court.


00:13:15.601 --> 00:13:17.226
It's called
a non-minor dependent court.


00:13:17.309 --> 00:13:21.310
It's for kids 18 to 21 that
have experienced foster care.


00:13:21.393 --> 00:13:23.268
Your judge is a real pioneer


00:13:23.352 --> 00:13:26.101
because she saw the statistics
of how bad it was


00:13:26.185 --> 00:13:28.769
for youth when they were leaving
the foster care system.


00:13:28.851 --> 00:13:32.353
So she has her very own court
dedicated to you guys.


00:13:32.436 --> 00:13:35.312
The other really cool thing
is that we have peer advocates,


00:13:35.395 --> 00:13:37.437
and do you mind telling her
what that's about?


00:13:37.521 --> 00:13:41.270
Yeah. We've all grown up
in, um, the foster care system,


00:13:41.354 --> 00:13:44.270
and I didn't get much support,


00:13:44.354 --> 00:13:46.979
so I just--
I wanted to be that person that,


00:13:47.063 --> 00:13:49.938
um, you know,
gave the resources and stuff,


00:13:50.021 --> 00:13:51.605
'cause it was hard.


00:13:51.688 --> 00:13:54.189
Can I ask?
And you don't have to answer,


00:13:54.272 --> 00:13:55.731
but, um, I'll ask,


00:13:55.814 --> 00:13:58.147
'cause it sounds like it's still
kinda hot for you,


00:13:58.231 --> 00:14:00.148
-very triggering, is--
-What?


00:14:00.232 --> 00:14:01.981
Is, like, um, your mom.


00:14:02.065 --> 00:14:04.065
Did you see or visit your mom
when you were little,


00:14:04.148 --> 00:14:07.273
-before you went home?
-Yeah, she was really nice.


00:14:07.357 --> 00:14:10.148
She was a nice lady,
and then I moved in,


00:14:10.232 --> 00:14:13.358
and first day,
she was sweet and everything.


00:14:13.441 --> 00:14:16.316
I remember the third day is
when it all started happening.


00:14:16.399 --> 00:14:18.692
But then I was there for like
a year and a half,


00:14:18.776 --> 00:14:22.567
and the cops showed up,
and they checked everybody.


00:14:22.651 --> 00:14:24.150
They checked our bodies
and everything,


00:14:24.234 --> 00:14:25.526
and I was the only one who,
like,


00:14:25.609 --> 00:14:27.151
had something from,
like, head to toe,


00:14:27.235 --> 00:14:29.652
covered in, like, bruises,
and cuts, and scars,


00:14:29.735 --> 00:14:30.901
and stuff like that.


00:14:30.984 --> 00:14:32.068
So, then they were just like,
"Oh,"


00:14:32.151 --> 00:14:33.610
and then they took us all away.


00:14:33.693 --> 00:14:36.610
Then we went to court,
and they put me on the stand,


00:14:36.693 --> 00:14:38.943
and they asked me to--
to share the story.


00:14:39.026 --> 00:14:41.445
Like, tell 'em what's going on.
What's going on at home?


00:14:41.529 --> 00:14:42.820
What's this? What's that?


00:14:42.903 --> 00:14:44.445
But my mom was sitting
right there in front me,


00:14:44.529 --> 00:14:46.362
and I couldn't--
I was terrified of her.


00:14:46.445 --> 00:14:48.737
So, I was like, "No, nothing,
nothing. Everything is fine.


00:14:48.820 --> 00:14:51.403
Good, good, good," right?
And, um...


00:14:51.487 --> 00:14:54.696
they put my sister up, Yesenia,
and then she said everything.


00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:58.238
And then they let us go
back home with her that day.


00:14:58.321 --> 00:14:59.530
I don't know why.


00:14:59.613 --> 00:15:01.112
But then one day,
out of nowhere,


00:15:01.196 --> 00:15:02.946
some social workers showed up,
and they were like,


00:15:03.029 --> 00:15:04.447
"All right, pack your stuff,"


00:15:04.531 --> 00:15:06.489
and then they took me,
and I went to my grandma's house


00:15:06.572 --> 00:15:08.073
and I never went back.


00:15:11.782 --> 00:15:14.448
I never understood her,
and I still don't.


00:15:15.406 --> 00:15:17.156
There were moments where, um...


00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:19.490
she liked to go clubbing
a lot...


00:15:19.573 --> 00:15:21.074
...and she'd come back drunk,


00:15:21.157 --> 00:15:23.533
and she'd hold me in her arms
and say that she was sorry


00:15:23.616 --> 00:15:25.658
and that everything was
gonna change starting today.


00:15:25.741 --> 00:15:27.659
"Everything is gonna
be different. I love you."


00:15:27.742 --> 00:15:28.950
And she'd hug me...


00:15:29.033 --> 00:15:30.492
...and she'd be listening
to music,


00:15:30.575 --> 00:15:32.325
and she'd be holding me there,
rocking me,


00:15:32.408 --> 00:15:34.618
and-- and I just--
I believed her,


00:15:34.701 --> 00:15:36.909
and I was like, "Love you,"
and I'd hug her and everything,


00:15:36.992 --> 00:15:39.243
and she'd let me sleep with her
on the bed one night.


00:15:39.326 --> 00:15:40.743
And then, the next morning,
she woke up.


00:15:40.826 --> 00:15:42.576
She was like, "What the...
are you doing here?"


00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:44.159
And she kicked me,
she slapped me,


00:15:44.243 --> 00:15:46.076
and me she kicked me out,
and she threw me out,


00:15:46.159 --> 00:15:47.785
and I was just so confused,
and I was like,


00:15:47.867 --> 00:15:51.245
I just-- I thought-- and then
she did that a ton of times.


00:15:51.328 --> 00:15:53.036
Um...


00:15:53.119 --> 00:15:54.578
But every time,
I just believed her.


00:15:54.662 --> 00:15:56.537
I just believed her.
I just believed her and...


00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:58.328
But it just never changed.


00:16:01.538 --> 00:16:02.870
Right here, Sydney.


00:16:02.954 --> 00:16:04.663
Get the edges
a little bit better, right here.


00:16:10.954 --> 00:16:13.120
I grew up in Arkansas,


00:16:13.204 --> 00:16:18.956
a small country town.
I'm the third of 18 children.


00:16:19.039 --> 00:16:21.456
I wanted to be like my mom,


00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.706
but I didn't want 18.
I'll settle for five.


00:16:25.872 --> 00:16:28.206
Five years,
I didn't get pregnant.


00:16:28.289 --> 00:16:30.040
I did everything
the doctor told me to do,


00:16:30.123 --> 00:16:32.624
and I couldn't get pregnant.
I thought, Lord,


00:16:32.707 --> 00:16:34.082
and I talked to my pastor.


00:16:34.165 --> 00:16:35.749
He said,
"I want you and your husband


00:16:35.832 --> 00:16:37.874
to come to communion,
and I'm gonna pray for you."


00:16:39.166 --> 00:16:41.375
Two weeks later, I was pregnant.


00:16:42.917 --> 00:16:44.459
God gave me a miracle baby.


00:16:44.543 --> 00:16:47.793
That's why I call my daughter,
Ericka, my miracle baby.


00:16:47.875 --> 00:16:50.875
And then, after I couldn't have
any more kids, my girlfriend,


00:16:50.959 --> 00:16:52.709
who worked for Department
of Children's Services,


00:16:52.793 --> 00:16:54.167
she said, "You love kids.


00:16:54.251 --> 00:16:55.918
Why don't you become
a foster parent?"


00:16:57.418 --> 00:17:01.378
Erika was seven
when I got my first kid,


00:17:01.461 --> 00:17:02.877
which was Steven.


00:17:05.086 --> 00:17:06.294
What time is it?


00:17:06.378 --> 00:17:08.002
Seven forty-five.


00:17:08.086 --> 00:17:10.212
Okay, the bus should be here
any minute.


00:17:11.504 --> 00:17:14.462
Casi has autism.


00:17:14.546 --> 00:17:18.128
Sweet little girl,
beautiful little girl,


00:17:18.212 --> 00:17:21.087
has a lot of problem that
she's not responsible for.


00:17:24.422 --> 00:17:26.464
-No, no.
-Come on. Come on. Come on.


00:17:26.964 --> 00:17:28.839
No.


00:17:28.922 --> 00:17:30.130
No.


00:17:38.131 --> 00:17:40.423
No, no, no, no, no!


00:17:40.507 --> 00:17:42.173
-Oh, God.


00:17:42.257 --> 00:17:44.423
Come on. Come on, Casi. Come on.


00:17:44.507 --> 00:17:45.799
No, no, no!


00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:51.883
No, no, no, no, no!


00:17:51.967 --> 00:17:53.551
Casi.


00:17:53.634 --> 00:17:55.467
Casi, you gotta get on the bus.


00:17:58.509 --> 00:18:00.967
Okay, the bus gotta go.
They're gonna go without you.


00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:02.509
-I'll go to the door.
-Yes, yeah!


00:18:02.592 --> 00:18:04.051
-Okay, come on.
-One step.


00:18:04.134 --> 00:18:06.260
Why are they yelling at me?


00:18:06.343 --> 00:18:08.426
One more step, Casi.


00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:09.593
Please?


00:18:09.677 --> 00:18:10.927
No.


00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:13.219
Do you think she'll be
all right on the bus?


00:18:13.302 --> 00:18:15.302
Yesterday, she bit me again.


00:18:16.427 --> 00:18:17.595
God.


00:18:17.679 --> 00:18:19.595
Oh, my God.


00:18:19.679 --> 00:18:21.095
Hope she'll be okay.


00:18:21.178 --> 00:18:23.011
-She'd never act this--


00:18:26.428 --> 00:18:27.428
You know what?


00:18:27.512 --> 00:18:29.178
Maybe I should just take her.


00:18:30.178 --> 00:18:32.596
-Come on, Casi.


00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:33.806
Come on, Casi.


00:18:33.888 --> 00:18:36.514
-Come on.


00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:44.431
-It's okay.


00:18:44.515 --> 00:18:47.848
I got you. Come on.
You're okay.


00:18:47.931 --> 00:18:49.723
Once I get them in school,


00:18:49.807 --> 00:18:51.223
get everybody in school,


00:18:51.306 --> 00:18:54.598
and get them situated,
then I can start my day.


00:18:54.682 --> 00:18:56.723
So, that's the purpose
of the morning prayer.


00:18:56.807 --> 00:18:58.267
So I guess


00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:00.517
I need to have prayed a little
bit harder for Casi.


00:19:16.017 --> 00:19:18.309
Majority of our children
that we see


00:19:18.393 --> 00:19:19.810
live in poverty.


00:19:19.892 --> 00:19:22.269
They are minorities.


00:19:22.352 --> 00:19:24.936
They come from
single-parent households.


00:19:25.019 --> 00:19:26.478
They come from families where


00:19:26.562 --> 00:19:29.812
there is undiagnosed
mental illness.


00:19:31.103 --> 00:19:33.353
These are the larger society's
problems


00:19:33.436 --> 00:19:35.103
that we haven't addressed.


00:19:37.103 --> 00:19:40.020
A majority of our cases
are general neglect.


00:19:40.104 --> 00:19:42.521
The children aren't being
properly cared for


00:19:42.604 --> 00:19:45.188
or provided for.


00:19:45.272 --> 00:19:48.230
We just don't get to choose
what families we're born into.


00:20:01.189 --> 00:20:03.939
I was actually born
into a pretty normal family.


00:20:04.022 --> 00:20:05.691
My parents were married.


00:20:05.774 --> 00:20:10.400
I have five siblings,
the usual cat, dog, and bike.


00:20:10.483 --> 00:20:13.858
Then when I was eight years old,
my mother filed for divorce,


00:20:13.941 --> 00:20:16.983
and just like that,
everything changed.


00:20:17.066 --> 00:20:18.734
In what seemed like
a matter of days,


00:20:18.818 --> 00:20:21.568
my family became
devastatingly poor.


00:20:21.651 --> 00:20:23.900
And somehow, in the mix
of all the commotion,


00:20:23.984 --> 00:20:26.234
my mom went to the doctor
and found out


00:20:26.317 --> 00:20:27.984
that she had breast cancer.


00:20:29.317 --> 00:20:31.819
I'm not sure what happened,


00:20:31.901 --> 00:20:34.278
but after a few treatments
of chemotherapy,


00:20:34.361 --> 00:20:35.695
she was a different person.


00:20:35.778 --> 00:20:37.902
She would beat us
and talk to us viciously


00:20:37.986 --> 00:20:40.069
for little to no reason.


00:20:40.152 --> 00:20:42.027
I remember being dragged
across the floor


00:20:42.111 --> 00:20:43.444
by my hair, naked,


00:20:43.528 --> 00:20:45.862
while being punched and kicked
all over my body,


00:20:47.779 --> 00:20:49.320
constantly being slapped
in the face,


00:20:49.404 --> 00:20:51.903
and called nasty whores
and bitches,


00:20:51.987 --> 00:20:54.613
and told to kill myself.


00:20:54.697 --> 00:20:57.154
I'm sorry. I've never told
this part of my story.


00:21:00.531 --> 00:21:02.698
My mom ended up getting
section eight housing,


00:21:02.781 --> 00:21:04.447
but the tragic thing is that


00:21:04.531 --> 00:21:06.072
when we actually did
get section eight,


00:21:06.155 --> 00:21:07.739
we moved into the hood,


00:21:07.823 --> 00:21:09.739
all hell really broke loose.


00:21:09.823 --> 00:21:14.365
My sixth grade experience
in my new school was terrifying.


00:21:14.448 --> 00:21:16.615
By the end of the first month
in our new school,


00:21:16.699 --> 00:21:18.783
both me and my big sister,
Charity,


00:21:18.866 --> 00:21:21.199
joined a clique,
which is a mini gang.


00:21:22.324 --> 00:21:23.949
I really liked being in a gang,


00:21:24.032 --> 00:21:27.200
because it gave me
a sense of normality,


00:21:27.284 --> 00:21:29.701
but mostly because I was afraid
of my mom,


00:21:29.784 --> 00:21:31.492
and after she would hit me
or degrade me,


00:21:31.576 --> 00:21:33.742
I could go hit someone else
and feel better,


00:21:33.826 --> 00:21:36.409
because after I discovered that
hitting people


00:21:36.492 --> 00:21:39.075
relieved some of the pain
that I was feeling inside,


00:21:39.158 --> 00:21:42.119
I stopped crying
when my mom would hit me.


00:21:42.202 --> 00:21:44.578
I didn't want her to use me
to feel better.


00:21:46.077 --> 00:21:47.994
The first time
I ran away from home--


00:21:48.077 --> 00:21:49.661
-Oops!


00:21:49.744 --> 00:21:51.952
That's my sister, Charity.
I'm gonna tell her, "Girl,


00:21:52.035 --> 00:21:53.327
don't be callin'
while I'm here."


00:21:53.411 --> 00:21:54.787
-She can feel it.


00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:56.495
She can feel I'm telling
the family business.


00:21:56.579 --> 00:21:58.662
She always knows. She's like,
"Stop tellin' our business!"


00:21:59.829 --> 00:22:02.078
She always knows
that I'm doin' it.


00:22:02.161 --> 00:22:03.705
But...


00:22:03.788 --> 00:22:05.954
So, actually, the first time
that I ran away from home,


00:22:06.037 --> 00:22:08.414
I remember my mother
was beating me in the kitchen,


00:22:08.497 --> 00:22:10.289
and I heard my big sister,
Charity,


00:22:10.372 --> 00:22:12.289
I believe, scream out, "Run."


00:22:13.622 --> 00:22:15.706
So, I ran
to my best friend's house,


00:22:15.789 --> 00:22:17.455
and after one look at me,


00:22:17.539 --> 00:22:21.582
her mom hid me from my mom
for about three days.


00:22:21.665 --> 00:22:23.623
During those three days
that I was there,


00:22:23.707 --> 00:22:26.791
I had mastered shoplifting
to a tee.


00:22:26.874 --> 00:22:30.207
One morning, after a successful
shoplifting spree,


00:22:30.291 --> 00:22:32.583
I was caught
by the grocery store security


00:22:32.666 --> 00:22:35.000
with two cartons eggs
and two packs of bacon


00:22:35.083 --> 00:22:38.542
stuffed under each of my arms
in my jacket.


00:22:38.625 --> 00:22:41.375
They called my mom
for over 12 hours


00:22:41.458 --> 00:22:45.208
before the executive decision
was made to call the police.


00:22:45.292 --> 00:22:47.041
I was taken to juvenile hall,


00:22:47.125 --> 00:22:49.251
and that's where
my foster care journey began.


00:23:01.002 --> 00:23:03.712
All right, let's meet over here.


00:23:03.795 --> 00:23:06.919
So, how are you feeling about
your court appearance today?


00:23:07.003 --> 00:23:08.378
All right. It's all good.


00:23:08.461 --> 00:23:10.086
Yeah?
You feel like you're doing well?


00:23:10.169 --> 00:23:11.336
Yeah, just--


00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:12.878
I just don't like court.
It just--


00:23:12.961 --> 00:23:14.212
What don't you like about court?


00:23:14.296 --> 00:23:15.755
It's just the pressure.


00:23:15.839 --> 00:23:17.505
-Yeah, stressful.
-Constant pressure, yeah.


00:23:17.589 --> 00:23:20.171
Okay. I have questions


00:23:20.255 --> 00:23:21.921
about especially last year.


00:23:22.005 --> 00:23:24.338
It sounded like a hard year.
Would you say?


00:23:24.422 --> 00:23:25.755
-A stressful year.
-Stressful year?


00:23:25.839 --> 00:23:27.797
I was never on probation
before last year.


00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:30.756
-Yup, yeah.
-Everything changed, really.


00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:33.089
Yeah, and it sounded like,
even the year before,


00:23:33.172 --> 00:23:34.506
a lot of changes happened,
right?


00:23:34.590 --> 00:23:35.673
-Mm-hmm.
-Like, you were in...


00:23:35.756 --> 00:23:37.048
four different group homes?


00:23:37.132 --> 00:23:38.507
Yeah, I was in
a lotta group homes.


00:23:38.591 --> 00:23:41.007
So, I wanna talk
a little bit about that.


00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:44.383
When you arrive in court here,
and they don't know you,


00:23:44.466 --> 00:23:45.633
and all they receive


00:23:45.717 --> 00:23:47.341
is something like
the police report,


00:23:47.425 --> 00:23:49.717
they see one act,


00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:53.216
and it's typically, you know,
in their eyes, a bad act,


00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:55.884
right? And so,
part of what my job is to do


00:23:55.967 --> 00:23:59.009
is, um, tell the fuller story
of Dasani.


00:23:59.092 --> 00:24:00.968
And so,
my way of working with you


00:24:01.051 --> 00:24:02.552
is to figure out what parts
of your story


00:24:02.635 --> 00:24:04.260
you actually want to share,


00:24:04.343 --> 00:24:06.968
but I don't wanna do it


00:24:07.051 --> 00:24:09.177
without your collaboration,
okay?


00:24:10.595 --> 00:24:11.761
Shall we go to court?


00:24:14.969 --> 00:24:16.720
Uh-uh.


00:24:16.803 --> 00:24:18.886
-What'd you say?
-Uh-uh, not back in there.


00:24:18.969 --> 00:24:20.344
-Uh-uh.
-Bad place.


00:24:20.428 --> 00:24:21.553
All right.


00:24:21.636 --> 00:24:22.762
Do you remember how long
you were in there?


00:24:22.846 --> 00:24:24.263
Approximately 25 days.


00:24:24.346 --> 00:24:26.221
-Twenty-- 25?
-It was the worst 25 days


00:24:26.305 --> 00:24:27.471
of my life.


00:24:27.555 --> 00:24:29.597
I think,
if you keep doing well,


00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:30.929
you won't end up back there.


00:24:52.099 --> 00:24:54.016
Let's bring in Dasani.


00:24:54.099 --> 00:24:56.182
We need to get
the APD to substitute.


00:24:56.266 --> 00:24:57.641
-No, I don't.
-No?


00:24:57.725 --> 00:24:59.141
Hello, Dasani.


00:24:59.224 --> 00:25:01.141
-How you doin', mister?
-I'm well. Thank you.


00:25:01.224 --> 00:25:03.892
So, Dasani, overall,
I got a great report.


00:25:03.975 --> 00:25:05.350
Your grades are really good.


00:25:05.434 --> 00:25:07.392
I see an A in Algebra,
a B in Life Skills,


00:25:07.475 --> 00:25:09.684
a B in History, and A in Lab.


00:25:09.768 --> 00:25:11.602
And how'd the C slip in there?
C in English?


00:25:11.685 --> 00:25:14.893
-How does that slip in there?
-Oh, I don't know. I mean...


00:25:14.976 --> 00:25:16.101
Come on. Let's work on that.


00:25:16.184 --> 00:25:17.269
Let's make sure
if you need tutoring,


00:25:17.352 --> 00:25:18.561
let's get tutoring in place.


00:25:18.644 --> 00:25:19.894
So, you're gonna be at school
on time.


00:25:19.977 --> 00:25:21.561
-Mm-hmm.
-Getting your work done.


00:25:21.644 --> 00:25:22.935
Getting your homework done,
right?


00:25:23.019 --> 00:25:24.894
-Yes, sir.
-You-- You're keeping your word.


00:25:24.977 --> 00:25:26.686
So, the only place you haven't


00:25:26.769 --> 00:25:28.102
is I got a negative test.


00:25:28.185 --> 00:25:31.729
I mean a positive test,
excuse me. 138 nanograms,


00:25:31.812 --> 00:25:34.313
which means you took
a couple hits. Yes?


00:25:34.396 --> 00:25:36.229
-Yes, sir.
-Your Honor, um,


00:25:36.313 --> 00:25:37.937
he's struggling with sobriety,


00:25:38.021 --> 00:25:41.479
and relapse can be part--
is sometimes part of recovery.


00:25:41.563 --> 00:25:43.479
It is, often.
So, what happened?


00:25:44.522 --> 00:25:47.272
My-- I don't-- One of my friends


00:25:47.355 --> 00:25:49.980
gave me a brownie.
I was unaware.


00:25:50.063 --> 00:25:51.272
Hey, come on.


00:25:51.355 --> 00:25:53.022
But I take full responsibility.
Yeah, yeah.


00:25:53.105 --> 00:25:54.772
-You were-- You knew.
-I didn't-- I really didn't--


00:25:54.856 --> 00:25:56.607
You're sophisticated enough
to know. Please don't play me.


00:25:56.690 --> 00:25:58.566
Yeah, I didn't-- I didn't know,
but I take full responsibility.


00:25:58.649 --> 00:25:59.982
I wasn't born yesterday.


00:26:00.065 --> 00:26:02.357
You know. When you took
the first bite, you knew.


00:26:02.441 --> 00:26:05.691
And now, the other question is,
you called him a friend.


00:26:05.774 --> 00:26:07.274
Now, this friend may be using,


00:26:07.357 --> 00:26:10.107
but the friend should respect
your decision not to use


00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:11.609
and should be supporting that.


00:26:11.692 --> 00:26:13.150
You may wanna assess
who you have as friends.


00:26:13.233 --> 00:26:14.775
-Who your friends are.
-Your Honor,


00:26:14.859 --> 00:26:16.358
by way of clarification,


00:26:16.442 --> 00:26:18.442
this is another young person
who lives at the group home.


00:26:18.525 --> 00:26:19.901
So he is a friend,


00:26:19.984 --> 00:26:21.610
but he also is living
at the same place as Dasani.


00:26:21.693 --> 00:26:23.234
Maybe we need to recategorize
that as an acquaintance.


00:26:24.736 --> 00:26:26.277
Your Honor,
I also just wanted to flag


00:26:26.360 --> 00:26:28.235
that the team,
the defense team and probation,


00:26:28.319 --> 00:26:30.485
will be working together
to identify


00:26:30.569 --> 00:26:31.985
more individualized counseling


00:26:32.068 --> 00:26:34.985
to address some of
the underlying needs that, uh,


00:26:35.068 --> 00:26:38.236
may be manifesting itself
in substance use.


00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:40.903
Um, as the court knows, um,


00:26:40.986 --> 00:26:45.279
Dasani has quite a, um,
traumatic childhood history,


00:26:45.362 --> 00:26:48.154
and some of that
seems to be unaddressed.


00:26:48.237 --> 00:26:49.529
So we would like to explore,


00:26:49.613 --> 00:26:51.029
-for instance...
-Maybe I'm a Neanderthal.


00:26:51.112 --> 00:26:53.196
-...trauma informed therapy.
-I'm not into it.


00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:55.280
I need you
to take care of business.


00:26:55.363 --> 00:26:56.905
Perform,
which is what you're doing.


00:26:56.988 --> 00:26:59.530
-Don't make excuses, right?
-Yes, sir.


00:26:59.614 --> 00:27:01.071
All right,
that's all I'm asking for.


00:27:01.155 --> 00:27:03.155
-Thank you, Dasani.
-Thank you, Your Honor.


00:27:03.238 --> 00:27:04.530
-Good work.
-Thank you, Your Honor.


00:27:04.614 --> 00:27:06.948
Thanks, everyone.
All right, next case.


00:27:17.282 --> 00:27:19.491
Deputy Garcia,
ready to rock and roll?


00:27:23.283 --> 00:27:24.825
Watch your fingers.


00:27:27.283 --> 00:27:28.825
All right, good afternoon.


00:27:28.908 --> 00:27:31.701
Let me hear from children's
counsel, parents' counsel,


00:27:31.784 --> 00:27:33.410
and then I'll last hear
from the county


00:27:33.493 --> 00:27:34.993
with respect to
detention issues.


00:27:35.076 --> 00:27:36.702
Your Honor,
on behalf of Kris'Lyn,


00:27:36.785 --> 00:27:39.702
we are asking for her to be
returned to her father today.


00:27:39.785 --> 00:27:41.869
Even with the department's
low burden,


00:27:41.951 --> 00:27:43.951
they haven't established
by prima facie evidence


00:27:44.035 --> 00:27:47.244
that there's risk
if Kris'Lyn were released today.


00:27:47.328 --> 00:27:49.286
The department will now be
testing the mother,


00:27:49.369 --> 00:27:51.411
can do unannounced visits
to make sure that


00:27:51.494 --> 00:27:54.120
father isn't allowing
unauthorized contact,


00:27:54.203 --> 00:27:56.829
and that she's not visiting
or caring for the baby


00:27:56.912 --> 00:27:58.162
if under the influence.


00:27:58.245 --> 00:27:59.705
On behalf of father,
Your Honor,


00:27:59.788 --> 00:28:01.705
father is asking
for the child to be,


00:28:01.788 --> 00:28:03.496
uh, placed with him today.


00:28:03.580 --> 00:28:06.079
Father did not know
about mother's drug use,


00:28:06.163 --> 00:28:09.872
and mother misinformed
the department.


00:28:09.954 --> 00:28:11.330
Um...


00:28:11.413 --> 00:28:13.831
Misinformed is not exactly
the right word, but, uh--


00:28:13.914 --> 00:28:15.456
Did not tell the department
of her drug use


00:28:15.539 --> 00:28:18.040
and did not tell father of--
of her drug use, as well.


00:28:18.123 --> 00:28:20.749
But father is in a position now
to care for the minor


00:28:20.832 --> 00:28:23.165
and he's asking for the minor
to be returned to him today.


00:28:23.248 --> 00:28:24.582
All right. Mom's counsel?


00:28:24.665 --> 00:28:27.458
I think my client made mistakes
on so many levels,


00:28:27.541 --> 00:28:29.166
and the court pointed out
something


00:28:29.249 --> 00:28:30.625
that I felt was important:


00:28:30.708 --> 00:28:33.458
that my client lied
to the department


00:28:33.541 --> 00:28:37.041
in not disclosing
the correct circumstances


00:28:37.124 --> 00:28:40.710
that led her to use drugs
and the full story,


00:28:40.793 --> 00:28:42.793
but when DCFS is involved,


00:28:42.877 --> 00:28:45.126
parents are afraid
to lose their child,


00:28:45.209 --> 00:28:46.501
so the stories that come out


00:28:46.585 --> 00:28:49.251
may not really reflect
what transpired.


00:28:49.335 --> 00:28:50.877
She's certainly
not the first parent


00:28:50.959 --> 00:28:52.752
that's not telling the truth
to the department.


00:28:52.835 --> 00:28:53.919
Exactly.


00:28:54.002 --> 00:28:55.419
So I don't think father
should be judged


00:28:55.502 --> 00:28:57.960
on misrepresentations
made by my client, and--


00:28:58.044 --> 00:28:59.461
What are you asking for?


00:28:59.544 --> 00:29:01.210
I'm asking for release
to the father.


00:29:01.294 --> 00:29:04.462
My client would not in any way
conduct herself


00:29:04.545 --> 00:29:06.712
in a way to sabotage
a release to the father.


00:29:06.796 --> 00:29:09.962
So, my client would continue
with the program that she is in,


00:29:10.046 --> 00:29:11.588
which is
Tarzana Treatment Center.


00:29:11.671 --> 00:29:13.046
She will continue testing.


00:29:13.129 --> 00:29:15.546
She is amenable
to supervised visits.


00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:19.004
Uh, she will be grateful
if father could be the monitor.


00:29:19.087 --> 00:29:20.756
So we are joining
father's attorney


00:29:20.839 --> 00:29:23.047
and minor's attorney
in making that request.


00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.922
I'm not happy
about the misrepresentations.


00:29:26.005 --> 00:29:27.423
We'll be charitable at that.


00:29:27.506 --> 00:29:29.964
And those even could potentially
be tied to the father.


00:29:30.048 --> 00:29:34.507
But the father works.
He's got strong family support.


00:29:34.591 --> 00:29:36.716
I do think,
under the circumstances,


00:29:36.799 --> 00:29:40.007
it is appropriate
to detain from the mother,


00:29:40.090 --> 00:29:41.507
and I think that it's
appropriate


00:29:41.591 --> 00:29:43.549
to release to the father
with conditions.


00:29:43.633 --> 00:29:45.591
The release, I think,
should be conditioned


00:29:45.674 --> 00:29:48.467
on the father staying with
the grandfather


00:29:48.550 --> 00:29:51.009
so that there's
some level of supervision.


00:29:51.092 --> 00:29:55.301
So, let's discuss visitation.
It should be monitored.


00:29:55.384 --> 00:29:59.468
Uh, mom can't breastfeed
till she's testing clean.


00:29:59.551 --> 00:30:02.636
Dad, although the child
is released to you,


00:30:02.719 --> 00:30:05.052
she remains under
the supervision of the court.


00:30:05.135 --> 00:30:06.761
You're also not to
move addresses


00:30:06.844 --> 00:30:08.552
without notifying
the social worker.


00:30:08.636 --> 00:30:10.636
And, also,
let's make sure we have


00:30:10.719 --> 00:30:12.093
a holiday visitation schedule


00:30:12.177 --> 00:30:13.928
so that the mother
can see the child


00:30:14.011 --> 00:30:16.054
on Christmas Eve and Christmas.


00:30:16.137 --> 00:30:18.638
-All right, thank you.
-Thank you, Your Honor.


00:30:21.596 --> 00:30:22.763
You remember our conversation


00:30:22.846 --> 00:30:24.512
about the origin
of this problem?


00:30:24.596 --> 00:30:26.220
-Right.
-You have to deal with that...


00:30:26.304 --> 00:30:27.304
-I know.
-...squarely.


00:30:27.387 --> 00:30:28.430
-Okay.
-Okay?


00:30:28.513 --> 00:30:29.847
-Thank you.
-Take care, okay?


00:30:29.930 --> 00:30:31.221
Okay.


00:30:31.305 --> 00:30:33.221
And nice working with you.
Okay, thanks, guys.


00:30:33.305 --> 00:30:35.972
This is your attorney's
direct dial extension.


00:30:36.056 --> 00:30:37.514
-Okay.


00:30:39.389 --> 00:30:42.641
This is a release slip,
shows that you have custody.


00:30:42.724 --> 00:30:45.098
Um, they're gonna call
the social worker


00:30:45.182 --> 00:30:46.557
and let the social worker know


00:30:46.641 --> 00:30:48.182
your daughter
was released to you.


00:30:48.265 --> 00:30:51.849
You can call her and arrange
to pick up your daughter, okay?


00:30:56.016 --> 00:30:58.058
Everything is okay, baby.


00:30:58.141 --> 00:30:59.559
See? I told you.


00:30:59.643 --> 00:31:01.434
-All right?
-Okay.


00:31:01.517 --> 00:31:03.434
Right? We got the best news
ever, right?


00:31:03.517 --> 00:31:04.975
-Yeah.
-Okay, then.


00:31:07.726 --> 00:31:11.310
Everything just seemed
to have hit us at one time.


00:31:11.393 --> 00:31:13.268
We lost our car, place,


00:31:13.352 --> 00:31:15.602
and me being pregnant,
being homeless,


00:31:15.685 --> 00:31:18.185
I hit rock bottom.


00:31:18.268 --> 00:31:20.560
It was like, dang,
take everything away from us


00:31:20.644 --> 00:31:23.687
to give us this blessing that
we've been wanting for years.


00:31:31.771 --> 00:31:33.771
I made some bad decisions,


00:31:33.854 --> 00:31:36.979
and I allowed that situation
to take over me.


00:31:38.396 --> 00:31:42.188
I used cocaine
the day before my water broke.


00:31:44.897 --> 00:31:48.064
I just told her,
"We in this together."


00:31:48.147 --> 00:31:50.482
I'm here for you just like
you've been here for me,


00:31:50.565 --> 00:31:54.231
but just don't-- don't make
another mistake like this again.


00:32:09.942 --> 00:32:13.192
-Butterfly.
-She's fussy, fussy.


00:32:13.275 --> 00:32:16.485
I have a bottle in the bag,
so you can make her a meal.


00:32:16.568 --> 00:32:18.234
Okay, thank you.


00:32:18.318 --> 00:32:21.610
Hey, beautiful.
There goes daddy baby.


00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:26.109
So, this is--
How are you with handling?


00:32:26.193 --> 00:32:27.276
Oh, I'm good.


00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:30.069
-It's okay.


00:32:30.152 --> 00:32:33.028
It's okay, beautiful.


00:32:33.111 --> 00:32:34.986
So, do you have a bed?


00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.195
-Bassinet.
-It's in there now?


00:32:37.278 --> 00:32:39.862
-No, it's, um-- Mm-hmm.
-So, it's all coming?


00:32:39.945 --> 00:32:42.070
Do you know around what time
she'll be here?


00:32:42.153 --> 00:32:45.237
She's-- I don't know.
About an hour.


00:32:45.321 --> 00:32:47.655
-What's wrong?


00:32:49.237 --> 00:32:50.279
Huh?


00:32:55.864 --> 00:32:57.490
-Hi.
-Oh, hi. How are ya?


00:32:57.573 --> 00:32:59.907
I'm Redina Sheriff,
the social worker.


00:32:59.989 --> 00:33:01.239
Okay, social worker.


00:33:01.323 --> 00:33:02.573
-Nice to meet you.
-Okay, how you doing?


00:33:02.657 --> 00:33:04.406
I'm gonna give you my card.


00:33:04.490 --> 00:33:06.156
So, the baby is gonna be where?


00:33:06.239 --> 00:33:08.823
Uh, I'm gonna have to
set her bassinet up in here.


00:33:08.907 --> 00:33:10.323
-Okay.
-Yeah.


00:33:10.406 --> 00:33:12.199
I'll probably just sleep
on the couch now.


00:33:17.032 --> 00:33:18.282
You're home now.


00:33:23.492 --> 00:33:26.034
I'm so happy I got you back.


00:33:31.785 --> 00:33:32.910
Ready?


00:33:33.910 --> 00:33:35.034
A one.


00:33:40.702 --> 00:33:43.578
Look at Jake.


00:33:48.620 --> 00:33:50.536
-Surprise!
-Hey, Jake.


00:33:50.620 --> 00:33:51.871
Hey, Mama.


00:33:53.954 --> 00:33:55.412
Have a nice weekend!


00:33:57.371 --> 00:33:58.788
We've been waiting on you.


00:33:58.871 --> 00:34:01.204
Yeah, for a very long time.


00:34:02.871 --> 00:34:06.039
-Are your friends on the bus?
-Yeah.


00:34:06.122 --> 00:34:07.456
Jake was three years old


00:34:07.539 --> 00:34:09.122
when I got Jake,


00:34:09.206 --> 00:34:10.997
and he had cerebral palsy.


00:34:11.081 --> 00:34:13.039
He had a lotta things
wrong with him,


00:34:13.122 --> 00:34:15.498
but he loved people.


00:34:15.581 --> 00:34:18.457
I remember he couldn't do
anything for himself.


00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:21.916
I had to bathe him.
I had to brush his teeth.


00:34:23.123 --> 00:34:25.082
I taught him how to walk.


00:34:25.165 --> 00:34:27.248
I taught him how to use
a knife and a fork


00:34:27.332 --> 00:34:30.333
and not to use his fingers
and eat.


00:34:30.416 --> 00:34:33.000
And then the social worker
came to me and said,


00:34:33.084 --> 00:34:35.167
"You have worked miracles
with this child.


00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:36.584
He can be adopted now.


00:34:36.668 --> 00:34:40.292
We wanna take him
and put him in an adopted home


00:34:40.376 --> 00:34:42.834
to get him ready for adoption."


00:34:42.918 --> 00:34:46.210
I said, "Are you crazy or what?
You're not taking my baby.


00:34:46.293 --> 00:34:49.835
I have worked with him for years
to get him to where he is now.


00:34:49.919 --> 00:34:52.795
Why would I let you take him?
You're not taking him.


00:34:52.878 --> 00:34:56.836
If anybody adopt him,
I will be the one to adopt him,


00:34:56.920 --> 00:34:59.045
but you guys can't take him
from me


00:34:59.128 --> 00:35:00.629
'cause I'm all he knows."


00:35:04.587 --> 00:35:08.629
I was in seven other
foster homes before this one.


00:35:11.921 --> 00:35:13.296
The difference is,


00:35:13.380 --> 00:35:15.129
in between this home
and the other homes,


00:35:15.213 --> 00:35:18.798
is I used to run away,
and now I don't run away.


00:35:20.339 --> 00:35:23.506
I would always throw stuff
around at people


00:35:23.589 --> 00:35:27.256
and, like,
just tore down things,


00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:30.674
and so I just don't do it
here anymore.


00:35:32.674 --> 00:35:36.006
Once a kid is taken
from their parent,


00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:38.006
if they didn't have
an issue before,


00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:39.634
they got one now,


00:35:39.717 --> 00:35:42.258
and that's what I try to tell
some of the social workers.


00:35:42.342 --> 00:35:45.467
I said, "Once you took
that kid away,


00:35:45.550 --> 00:35:47.509
they're gonna become bitter.


00:35:47.592 --> 00:35:50.008
They're gonna become
destructive.


00:35:50.092 --> 00:35:51.592
They're gonna become angry.


00:35:51.676 --> 00:35:53.551
They're gonna have
a lotta problem wrong with them


00:35:53.635 --> 00:35:56.885
because you took them
from their comfort zone,


00:35:56.968 --> 00:35:58.426
from their parents,


00:35:58.510 --> 00:36:01.468
from their school,
from their friends.


00:36:01.551 --> 00:36:04.928
You took them away,
and you expect 'em to be


00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:06.845
okay with that. They're not."


00:36:14.136 --> 00:36:17.470
When I turned 18,
I thought I was ready.


00:36:17.553 --> 00:36:23.137
I was excited. I was like,
I'm gonna do my own thing now.


00:36:23.221 --> 00:36:25.179
The social workers
would often say,


00:36:25.262 --> 00:36:26.597
"Oh, you know what?


00:36:26.681 --> 00:36:28.347
You're one of our
most successful kids."


00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:30.806
I just feel like I have to
meet up to their expectations.


00:36:30.889 --> 00:36:32.097
I have to do good.


00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:33.640
If I get a C in a class,
that's not good enough.


00:36:33.723 --> 00:36:34.890
I feel like I have to do better.


00:36:37.431 --> 00:36:38.682
There you go.


00:36:40.765 --> 00:36:42.431
In the system,


00:36:42.515 --> 00:36:45.223
you hear a lot about kids


00:36:45.306 --> 00:36:46.807
who go into homes where,
you know,


00:36:46.890 --> 00:36:48.307
they're sexually abused,


00:36:48.391 --> 00:36:51.350
even physically, verbally,
any kind of abuse, um,


00:36:51.433 --> 00:36:53.058
you can think of. Um...


00:36:53.141 --> 00:36:57.725
These parents, foster parents,
however you want to call them,


00:36:57.809 --> 00:36:59.684
um, sometimes the parents
don't know


00:36:59.767 --> 00:37:01.351
what the child has
been through, or they do,


00:37:01.434 --> 00:37:04.893
and, you know, they'll send
the kids to therapy,


00:37:04.976 --> 00:37:07.726
take medications, the, um, um...


00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:10.685
And just-- or the kid is
too much for them to handle,


00:37:10.768 --> 00:37:12.310
so then they--
they send them off.


00:37:12.394 --> 00:37:15.645
So, it's like-- it's--
it's delicate. It's...


00:37:15.728 --> 00:37:17.019
Um, okay...


00:37:17.103 --> 00:37:19.436
College was nothing
like I expected.


00:37:20.895 --> 00:37:23.103
It's a lot harder
than I thought it would be.


00:37:24.603 --> 00:37:26.436
I think having been born
a drug baby


00:37:26.520 --> 00:37:29.604
has definitely affected
the way I learn.


00:37:31.104 --> 00:37:34.604
It's hard for me
to process things.


00:37:34.688 --> 00:37:38.355
It's hard for me to focus.
It makes me feel dumb.


00:37:39.897 --> 00:37:41.398
I want to be able to handle it.


00:37:41.481 --> 00:37:43.690
I want to be able to
get my stuff in order, but...


00:37:45.815 --> 00:37:46.940
I'm not ready.


00:37:53.981 --> 00:37:55.982
-Where were you born?
-Chicago.


00:37:56.065 --> 00:37:57.857
And how long
did you live in Chicago?


00:37:57.941 --> 00:38:00.566
I was there for, like,
five, six years.


00:38:00.650 --> 00:38:04.149
Okay. What do you
remember about Chicago as a kid?


00:38:04.233 --> 00:38:06.191
Not much.
Yeah, I was a little kid.


00:38:06.274 --> 00:38:09.067
I mean, I remember streets.


00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:10.693
Okay.


00:38:10.776 --> 00:38:12.442
My mom used to walk me
to school when I was little,


00:38:12.526 --> 00:38:13.651
as a toddler.


00:38:13.734 --> 00:38:14.901
I remember that street.


00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:17.734
What were the streets like?


00:38:22.861 --> 00:38:24.277
That's...


00:38:24.361 --> 00:38:26.069
You're telling me
something with that look,


00:38:26.152 --> 00:38:28.194
-but I-- I can't translate it!
-As-- As in-- As in--


00:38:28.277 --> 00:38:29.486
What you-- What-- What kinda--


00:38:29.569 --> 00:38:31.820
What are you asking
about the street?


00:38:31.903 --> 00:38:33.903
Like, do you have
good memories of the streets,


00:38:33.986 --> 00:38:36.195
or do you have bad
memories of the streets?


00:38:36.278 --> 00:38:37.696
Both good and bad.


00:38:37.779 --> 00:38:40.195
Okay, give me
an example of the good.


00:38:41.529 --> 00:38:43.987
-I liked the weather.
-Okay.


00:38:45.195 --> 00:38:48.989
And that's not really
a lot about...


00:38:52.239 --> 00:38:54.114
Not a lot about
the streets that were good?


00:38:54.197 --> 00:38:55.364
-It-- It--
-Okay.


00:38:55.447 --> 00:38:57.781
It's--
It's not so much as that as...


00:38:58.739 --> 00:38:59.864
Ooh.


00:39:07.073 --> 00:39:08.700
I'm getting memories.


00:39:08.783 --> 00:39:10.825
What's coming to your mind?


00:39:10.908 --> 00:39:12.366
My little brother.


00:39:16.075 --> 00:39:17.117
Oh.


00:39:19.826 --> 00:39:22.200
-Can we stop, please?
-Mm-hmm.


00:39:22.283 --> 00:39:25.617
I'm gettin' that-- that--
that good glory sweat, dude.


00:39:25.701 --> 00:39:26.909
Right.


00:39:33.744 --> 00:39:36.327
Dasani did witness his mother
being murdered.


00:39:36.411 --> 00:39:38.619
-Mm-hmm.
-By a father figure.


00:39:38.703 --> 00:39:41.661
It's unclear whether that
was mom's boyfriend


00:39:41.744 --> 00:39:43.745
or whether it was
the biological father.


00:39:43.829 --> 00:39:44.954
When he tells the story,


00:39:45.036 --> 00:39:46.370
he says that his dad
killed his mom.


00:39:46.453 --> 00:39:48.745
-Right.
-Okay. And then, at that point,


00:39:48.829 --> 00:39:51.036
the father that he knew
gets incarcerated.


00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:52.620
Mom is now deceased,


00:39:52.704 --> 00:39:56.037
and he gets placed with
a 60-year-old family friend.


00:39:56.121 --> 00:39:58.288
And gives him up in 2014.


00:39:58.372 --> 00:39:59.747
Yes.


00:39:59.831 --> 00:40:01.497
Then he ends up
in a number of group homes.


00:40:01.580 --> 00:40:02.872
-Then he's bouncing around...
-Right. Right.


00:40:02.956 --> 00:40:04.414
...from group home
to group home.


00:40:04.497 --> 00:40:06.664
What I hadn't
delved into as deeply


00:40:06.747 --> 00:40:08.622
is the ten years he was
with his adopted parent.


00:40:08.706 --> 00:40:10.163
-Mm-hmm.
-She physically abused him.


00:40:10.248 --> 00:40:12.206
He said that the foster mother
burned him,


00:40:12.289 --> 00:40:15.498
and he's got a burn mark,
with an iron. She denied it.


00:40:16.456 --> 00:40:18.541
And when she decided to


00:40:18.624 --> 00:40:20.708
call DCFS and say,
"I don't want him anymore"--


00:40:20.791 --> 00:40:22.416
She said,
"And I think, at this point,


00:40:22.499 --> 00:40:23.875
I just need to let him go."


00:40:23.959 --> 00:40:25.917
-Mm-hmm.
-That "he's a big boy now."


00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:27.750
-Mm-hmm.
-He was like 13, 14.


00:40:27.834 --> 00:40:29.500
No, they couldn't
pick him up quick enough.


00:40:29.583 --> 00:40:31.125
So she took all of his clothes,


00:40:31.208 --> 00:40:34.500
put them in trash bags,
and dumped him off at DCFS.


00:40:34.583 --> 00:40:37.918
Well, I wish I had gotten
on the case earlier,


00:40:38.001 --> 00:40:41.752
but I'm really grateful that
we have collaboration here


00:40:41.836 --> 00:40:44.085
because I think
it's the case management,


00:40:44.168 --> 00:40:46.377
your relationship with Dasani,


00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:49.502
and all the handling
of the social work,


00:40:49.585 --> 00:40:53.336
the placement that makes
getting off probation quicker.


00:40:53.420 --> 00:40:55.795
-Mm-hmm.
-Dasani is a foster youth.


00:40:55.878 --> 00:40:56.920
I mean, this is--


00:40:57.003 --> 00:40:58.837
His delinquency is a product
of that.


00:40:58.920 --> 00:41:01.920
They recognize that a lot
of his actions are trauma.


00:41:02.003 --> 00:41:03.712
So, he really did,


00:41:03.795 --> 00:41:06.005
and these kids really do
fall through the cracks.


00:41:06.088 --> 00:41:07.922
I think he fell
through a major crack.


00:41:08.005 --> 00:41:09.130
Right.


00:41:11.213 --> 00:41:13.380
The tough thing
about the system


00:41:13.463 --> 00:41:15.171
is that it is a system.


00:41:15.255 --> 00:41:17.755
Los Angeles County
is a massive county.


00:41:17.839 --> 00:41:22.464
So it's like trying to treat
everybody the same,


00:41:22.548 --> 00:41:25.214
but everyone is not the same.
Everyone's unique.


00:41:27.214 --> 00:41:30.215
It's kinda maxed out.
It's at capacity.


00:41:30.298 --> 00:41:31.967
So, a lot of these
social workers,


00:41:32.049 --> 00:41:36.591
they aren't able to go
the extra mile for every case.


00:41:40.091 --> 00:41:41.383
Foster youth,


00:41:41.466 --> 00:41:43.299
they're in trouble,
they're on probation,


00:41:43.383 --> 00:41:46.175
and everyone has a judgement
on the youth,


00:41:46.259 --> 00:41:48.509
and what they did,
and what they should do,


00:41:48.592 --> 00:41:52.093
but nobody is with them
day to day.


00:42:02.094 --> 00:42:04.011
I spent most
of my teenage years


00:42:04.094 --> 00:42:05.803
in between
lockdown group homes,


00:42:05.886 --> 00:42:09.219
juvenile hall, and staying
with friends and boyfriends.


00:42:14.262 --> 00:42:15.513
As a probation youth,


00:42:15.596 --> 00:42:17.138
when you run away
from placement,


00:42:17.221 --> 00:42:19.930
they put a warrant out
for your arrest.


00:42:20.013 --> 00:42:22.388
I just kept on AWOLing,
get locked up,


00:42:22.471 --> 00:42:24.096
AWOL, get locked up, AWOL.


00:42:29.723 --> 00:42:31.806
I don't know if I was
really depressed or indifferent,


00:42:31.889 --> 00:42:34.347
but I just don't remember
any light, like, ever,


00:42:34.431 --> 00:42:35.681
besides, like, the...


00:42:35.764 --> 00:42:37.389
I don't remember ever seeing,
like, light.


00:42:37.472 --> 00:42:39.015
It's always been open like that.


00:42:39.098 --> 00:42:40.224
-It's always been open?
-Mm-hmm.


00:42:40.307 --> 00:42:41.683
I was in eternal darkness.


00:42:44.641 --> 00:42:46.891
Do you remember
when you first got here?


00:42:46.975 --> 00:42:49.099
You didn't wanna interact
with the other kids.


00:42:49.182 --> 00:42:50.474
You didn't like the staff.


00:42:50.558 --> 00:42:52.057
You didn't wanna
come out your room.


00:42:52.141 --> 00:42:55.017
You didn't like the food.
You didn't like the shower.


00:42:56.600 --> 00:42:58.767
I think a lot of it
is for safety reasons


00:42:58.851 --> 00:43:02.226
that they have the open-stalled
restrooms and showers,


00:43:02.309 --> 00:43:03.893
but, you know, I'm a kid.


00:43:03.977 --> 00:43:06.436
I was embarrassed,
I was humiliated,


00:43:06.519 --> 00:43:09.686
and I didn't want them to see
inside of me or over me.


00:43:09.769 --> 00:43:11.144
And, you know,
they used to laugh


00:43:11.227 --> 00:43:13.185
'cause I used be the one,
like, walking like this.


00:43:15.185 --> 00:43:17.352
To me, it was so degrading


00:43:17.436 --> 00:43:21.353
and so awful that...


00:43:21.437 --> 00:43:23.687
I don't know. It's just really
hard to care about yourself


00:43:23.770 --> 00:43:25.688
when you're being punished
at that level.


00:43:26.771 --> 00:43:28.271
I saw in Jessica


00:43:28.354 --> 00:43:31.980
a person that was crying out
for help.


00:43:32.062 --> 00:43:34.272
She didn't realize
she was screaming, "Help me,"


00:43:34.355 --> 00:43:36.939
but I-- I felt it, and I saw it.


00:43:37.022 --> 00:43:39.397
I think she had been told
many times


00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:41.939
that she was never gonna amount
to anything.


00:43:42.022 --> 00:43:44.689
Nobody made her feel like
she was worth anything.


00:43:44.772 --> 00:43:47.064
"You're never gonna be anything.
Nobody cares."


00:43:47.148 --> 00:43:50.607
You know, but I saw something
in her that--


00:43:50.691 --> 00:43:52.232
that told me different,


00:43:52.315 --> 00:43:54.232
and if somebody can reach her,


00:43:54.315 --> 00:43:56.733
this could be one of
the kids that we can save.


00:43:59.065 --> 00:44:00.608
So, Jessica,


00:44:00.692 --> 00:44:03.150
what really helped you achieve
the success that you've had,


00:44:03.233 --> 00:44:06.650
and why do you believe
those forces around you worked?


00:44:06.734 --> 00:44:08.358
The biggest thing, I think,


00:44:08.442 --> 00:44:12.484
was having people come into
my life and hold hope for me.


00:44:12.568 --> 00:44:15.277
There was very long
periods of time


00:44:15.360 --> 00:44:17.944
in my adolescence
where I had given up,


00:44:18.027 --> 00:44:21.819
um, on believing
that I could achieve anything.


00:44:21.902 --> 00:44:25.110
When I was 18, I went to
a nonprofit organization,


00:44:25.193 --> 00:44:27.318
and I met people there
who started talkin' to me


00:44:27.402 --> 00:44:30.069
for the first time
about education,


00:44:30.153 --> 00:44:33.153
and at that time,
I had very firm beliefs


00:44:33.236 --> 00:44:36.195
that I was a stupid person.


00:44:36.279 --> 00:44:38.362
I tested very low
in community college.


00:44:38.446 --> 00:44:41.613
I tested into third grade math
and eighth grade English,


00:44:41.697 --> 00:44:43.488
which was very discouraging,


00:44:43.572 --> 00:44:46.822
but they believed in me,
and I really needed that


00:44:46.905 --> 00:44:49.864
because even after they
discovered that I was pregnant,


00:44:49.947 --> 00:44:52.321
and I thought that they were
gonna give up on me


00:44:52.405 --> 00:44:54.656
'cause I wasn't gonna
become a success story,


00:44:54.740 --> 00:44:57.156
they still pushed me.


00:44:57.239 --> 00:44:59.532
People have spoken life
into me that--


00:44:59.615 --> 00:45:01.449
and-- and I was dead.


00:45:02.907 --> 00:45:07.198
-Jessica Antonia Chandler.


00:45:07.282 --> 00:45:10.533
No crying, baby.
This is happy. This is happy.


00:45:10.616 --> 00:45:13.950
Mom! Go Mom!


00:45:15.450 --> 00:45:18.366
Two of my former
foster sisters,


00:45:18.450 --> 00:45:19.950
when they found me on Facebook


00:45:20.033 --> 00:45:22.744
and saw that I had got
my master's degree,


00:45:22.827 --> 00:45:24.452
they decided to go to college.


00:45:24.535 --> 00:45:27.243
I guess I was just so terrible
that they were like,


00:45:27.326 --> 00:45:30.076
"If Jessica got a diploma,
if she graduated from college,


00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:31.285
anybody can.


00:45:31.368 --> 00:45:32.827
Like, this must be easy."


00:45:32.910 --> 00:45:35.745
And even thought it wasn't easy,
it wasn't--


00:45:35.828 --> 00:45:37.077
since they knew my story,


00:45:37.161 --> 00:45:39.453
and they knew
how much I had struggled


00:45:39.536 --> 00:45:42.119
and how bad my behaviors had
been at one time,


00:45:42.202 --> 00:45:45.120
they knew that transformation
was possible for them, too.


00:46:07.414 --> 00:46:09.290
-Hi.
-Hi, Miss Redina.


00:46:09.373 --> 00:46:11.123
-How are you?
-Good. How are you?


00:46:11.206 --> 00:46:13.582
Good, good.
Do you wanna go into the room?


00:46:13.665 --> 00:46:15.081
Yes, please.


00:46:15.165 --> 00:46:17.791
Here's baby.


00:46:17.875 --> 00:46:19.124
-Oh!


00:46:19.207 --> 00:46:23.041
Here's mommy.
Here's my mommy.


00:46:23.124 --> 00:46:25.332
-Hi, babe.
-Hi, beautiful.


00:46:28.166 --> 00:46:31.125
Here's your mama.


00:46:31.208 --> 00:46:34.084
And Merry Christmas.
This for her.


00:46:34.168 --> 00:46:35.418
Thank you. Thank you.


00:46:35.501 --> 00:46:37.835
So you can get more
little baby items.


00:46:37.918 --> 00:46:39.334
Here's your mommy, baby.


00:46:39.418 --> 00:46:41.334
I was gonna go
right to Walmart, too, after.


00:46:41.418 --> 00:46:44.085
-Oh, okay, that's good.
-Thank you. I appreciate it.


00:46:44.169 --> 00:46:48.544
Your mommy missed you. Aww.


00:46:48.627 --> 00:46:50.878
Miss Sheriff got you a present.


00:46:53.003 --> 00:46:55.336
-What's the matter?
-You're probably wondering about


00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:57.379
-the whole monitoring...


00:46:57.463 --> 00:46:58.838
-...who can monitor.
-Yeah.


00:46:58.921 --> 00:47:02.337
And then how, for father,
I'm gonna provide


00:47:02.421 --> 00:47:05.671
family reunification services.
Um...


00:47:05.755 --> 00:47:06.963
What does that mean?


00:47:07.046 --> 00:47:08.880
They come once a week,


00:47:08.963 --> 00:47:12.213
and they'll address
any of your issues,


00:47:12.297 --> 00:47:14.756
and they have
quarterly meetings,


00:47:14.839 --> 00:47:16.797
and you would go to the meeting,


00:47:16.881 --> 00:47:19.715
and we'll discuss
what we've accomplished,


00:47:19.798 --> 00:47:22.048
-what we still need to do...
-What needs--


00:47:22.131 --> 00:47:23.423
...and so forth.


00:47:23.507 --> 00:47:26.466
-To get us back together?
-Right.


00:47:26.549 --> 00:47:29.049
That's our number one thing:


00:47:29.132 --> 00:47:33.382
to reunify families because,
really and truly,


00:47:33.466 --> 00:47:36.090
we don't wanna see you
back in the department.


00:47:36.174 --> 00:47:38.383
And I don't want to
come back.


00:47:38.467 --> 00:47:40.508
-And so, in order--
-I wanna come back


00:47:40.592 --> 00:47:42.134
and show you
how good we're doin'.


00:47:42.217 --> 00:47:43.384
-Right.
-Right.


00:47:43.468 --> 00:47:44.843
And how much progress
we're making.


00:47:44.926 --> 00:47:46.551
See the progress
that you're doin'.


00:47:46.634 --> 00:47:48.676
Right, right, right.
And that's understandable.


00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:50.051
I miss both of them,


00:47:50.134 --> 00:47:52.302
'cause it's not just her
that's taken from me.


00:47:52.385 --> 00:47:55.343
It's him, too, right now.
So it's hard.


00:47:57.469 --> 00:47:59.427
-How long is our visit?
-And--


00:47:59.510 --> 00:48:02.260
I can allow for two hours.


00:48:02.343 --> 00:48:04.344
-Is that fine?
-Yeah.


00:48:06.013 --> 00:48:07.929
I'm gonna give you guys
a little time, okay?


00:48:08.013 --> 00:48:09.054
Okay, thank you.


00:48:10.262 --> 00:48:11.429
Thank you, Miss Redina.


00:48:11.512 --> 00:48:14.095
Yeah, I'll be back
to check on you.


00:48:14.179 --> 00:48:15.429
Thank you.


00:48:17.304 --> 00:48:19.764
-I miss you.
-I miss you, too, baby.


00:48:25.472 --> 00:48:27.263
How has she been
through the night?


00:48:27.346 --> 00:48:29.431
I got an alarm
for every three hours...


00:48:30.765 --> 00:48:32.265
...but it's-- it been all right.


00:48:32.348 --> 00:48:34.140
She eats every three hours?


00:48:34.223 --> 00:48:35.265
Yeah.


00:48:35.348 --> 00:48:36.932
-She's eatin' a lot?
-Yeah.


00:48:40.223 --> 00:48:41.891
Look at all that hair.


00:48:48.516 --> 00:48:49.892
I didn't grow up


00:48:49.975 --> 00:48:52.476
with social services
involved in my life.


00:48:52.559 --> 00:48:56.267
I did grow up in
a domestic violence household.


00:48:56.350 --> 00:48:58.435
I was always the one
to go take the kids,


00:48:58.518 --> 00:49:00.310
and go in the room,
and close the door,


00:49:00.393 --> 00:49:01.477
and shelter them,


00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:03.977
and tell them not to listen,
and...


00:49:04.060 --> 00:49:06.393
The only thing
that I got out of that


00:49:06.477 --> 00:49:09.435
was to not tolerate
any man to hit me.


00:49:09.518 --> 00:49:11.895
That was one of my main things
growing up,


00:49:11.978 --> 00:49:13.811
when I'd seen them
go through that,


00:49:13.895 --> 00:49:16.645
that this is what I don't want,


00:49:16.729 --> 00:49:19.645
and so when I, you know,
when I got with Chris,


00:49:19.729 --> 00:49:22.687
he gave me that.


00:50:04.817 --> 00:50:07.193
My mom had me when she was 15.


00:50:07.276 --> 00:50:08.777
She's a single parent.


00:50:09.902 --> 00:50:12.526
I seen stuff a three-year-old,


00:50:12.610 --> 00:50:14.526
four-year-old shouldn't have
never seen.


00:50:16.318 --> 00:50:18.443
My mom and my dad was arguin',


00:50:18.526 --> 00:50:21.277
and my mom was saying,
"Don't leave. Don't leave."


00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:25.320
My dad went to the front door,
and he put a dollar in my hand,


00:50:25.403 --> 00:50:26.820
and then he left.


00:50:26.904 --> 00:50:28.570
Then I looked back
in the kitchen,


00:50:28.653 --> 00:50:30.029
and then I see my mom,


00:50:30.111 --> 00:50:33.988
um... trying to kill herself.


00:50:34.988 --> 00:50:37.738
Um, and, uh...


00:50:37.821 --> 00:50:40.404
that right there,
that scarred me for life.


00:50:40.488 --> 00:50:43.404
Growing up, I--
I couldn't be like my dad.


00:50:43.488 --> 00:50:44.654
I couldn't be like him.


00:50:51.406 --> 00:50:52.865
My mom always struggled.


00:50:52.948 --> 00:50:55.032
She struggled
after my father left us.


00:50:55.114 --> 00:50:56.448
She did her best,
but, you know,


00:50:56.531 --> 00:50:58.239
she-- she just couldn't do it.


00:50:59.531 --> 00:51:00.949
She's going crazy


00:51:01.033 --> 00:51:02.699
'cause she don't know how
she's gonna pay the rent,


00:51:02.783 --> 00:51:05.491
so... started hustlin'
on the streets.


00:51:06.783 --> 00:51:08.949
Been hustlin' since I was 11,


00:51:09.033 --> 00:51:12.075
stealing cars,
selling them to chop shops,


00:51:12.158 --> 00:51:15.326
and helping my mom pay rent.


00:51:15.409 --> 00:51:17.576
My number-one rule was,
I asked my mom not to--


00:51:17.659 --> 00:51:20.159
never ask me
where I got the money from.


00:51:20.242 --> 00:51:22.284
Just pay the bills.


00:51:22.367 --> 00:51:25.076
My mom always drilled in my
head she wanted me to stop,


00:51:25.159 --> 00:51:28.494
but it was just I was-- I was--
I was addicted to money.


00:51:28.577 --> 00:51:30.619
It was something that I--
I didn't grow up having,


00:51:30.702 --> 00:51:33.452
so it was, "I want that
instead of studying in school,"


00:51:33.535 --> 00:51:37.328
until I went to jail,
and I realized, uh, you know,


00:51:37.411 --> 00:51:39.578
I was-- I felt that
I was being selfish.


00:51:39.661 --> 00:51:41.496
I wasn't caring about my mom.


00:51:41.579 --> 00:51:43.621
I wasn't caring
that I'm the oldest son,


00:51:43.704 --> 00:51:45.537
and I have three little sisters.


00:51:48.996 --> 00:51:52.120
So, that-- that made me
want to change my ways.


00:51:53.662 --> 00:51:57.246
I see myself
sort of in my mom's shoes.


00:51:58.873 --> 00:52:01.331
Me struggling to try to
take care of my family


00:52:01.414 --> 00:52:04.289
like a-- like a man should
take care of his family.


00:52:20.707 --> 00:52:22.918
This is where Zoila lives,


00:52:23.001 --> 00:52:25.375
and I lived here
for seven years.


00:52:30.918 --> 00:52:32.542
Mary!


00:52:32.626 --> 00:52:34.542
-Hi, guys.
-Hi.


00:52:34.626 --> 00:52:37.002
I don't wanna
start hugging you.


00:52:37.085 --> 00:52:38.293
How are you?


00:52:39.543 --> 00:52:41.752
How are you? Hi, Babba.


00:52:44.461 --> 00:52:46.920
Mary was a...
little skinny little girl.


00:52:47.003 --> 00:52:49.587
She came when she was seven.


00:52:49.670 --> 00:52:54.754
She got along with my kids
and with my other foster kids.


00:52:54.837 --> 00:52:56.545
We just loved her to pieces.


00:52:58.046 --> 00:52:59.962
-The social worker said


00:53:00.046 --> 00:53:03.922
that she had been in 12 houses
prior to mine,


00:53:04.005 --> 00:53:05.505
and, to me, it was like,


00:53:05.588 --> 00:53:08.172
I'm gonna make this
the final home for her.


00:53:09.089 --> 00:53:10.839
I wanted her to stay here,


00:53:10.923 --> 00:53:14.297
and I wanted to see her
get married and move on,


00:53:14.380 --> 00:53:16.590
but it didn't work that way.


00:53:16.673 --> 00:53:19.298
-You didn't go to school today?


00:53:21.256 --> 00:53:23.049
-Shame on you.


00:53:23.131 --> 00:53:25.673
You've gotta be motivated
to go to school.


00:53:25.757 --> 00:53:29.674
That's the only way that you'll
be able to finish your classes.


00:53:30.675 --> 00:53:32.467
I know.


00:53:32.550 --> 00:53:36.717
-So, you started off good.
-I know.


00:53:36.801 --> 00:53:39.801
Even though she lived
with me for so many years,


00:53:39.884 --> 00:53:41.926
some of my rules
here at the house


00:53:42.009 --> 00:53:44.802
were rules that she didn't
wanna accept,


00:53:44.885 --> 00:53:47.635
and so
she wanted to try it out,


00:53:47.718 --> 00:53:49.134
um, elsewhere,


00:53:49.218 --> 00:53:51.301
to see if maybe
they didn't have any rules


00:53:51.384 --> 00:53:52.885
as strict as I was.


00:53:52.968 --> 00:53:54.803
Oh, she was okay. She's...


00:53:54.886 --> 00:53:56.678
you know, we don't have
no problem with her.


00:53:56.762 --> 00:53:58.804
But I was always in trouble,
remember?


00:53:58.887 --> 00:54:02.386
Yeah, but, um,
I-- I-- I cannot say nothing.


00:54:02.470 --> 00:54:04.678
She's in trouble
because she wants to do it.


00:54:05.762 --> 00:54:07.220
Like I always told her,


00:54:07.303 --> 00:54:10.679
I see her as my daughter,
and I want the best.


00:54:10.763 --> 00:54:14.554
So, when I see her doing
something that wasn't right,


00:54:14.638 --> 00:54:17.179
I would always call it
to her attention,


00:54:17.263 --> 00:54:20.764
and I think that's where our
relationship was breaking up.


00:54:23.556 --> 00:54:25.431
It was very difficult.


00:54:25.515 --> 00:54:28.264
I cried, and she cried, and,


00:54:28.348 --> 00:54:30.223
you know, but I think that,
at that time,


00:54:30.306 --> 00:54:32.057
it was the best thing
for both of us.


00:54:32.139 --> 00:54:33.556
I didn't really think
I was gonna do it.


00:54:33.640 --> 00:54:36.348
I was just being
a teenager rebel.


00:54:36.431 --> 00:54:37.682
I just wanted to leave.


00:54:37.766 --> 00:54:39.557
But then when I was actually
in the new home,


00:54:39.641 --> 00:54:41.391
and I arrived,
and I had my luggage,


00:54:41.475 --> 00:54:43.308
then I was in, like,
a little room,


00:54:43.391 --> 00:54:45.100
I just looked around,
and I was like,


00:54:45.183 --> 00:54:47.059
"What the heck just happened?"


00:54:47.141 --> 00:54:48.600
And I just started crying,


00:54:48.683 --> 00:54:50.183
and then I called my mom
right away,


00:54:50.266 --> 00:54:52.726
and I was like,
"I miss you. I wanna go back."


00:54:52.810 --> 00:54:54.768
It was really hard.
It took me a while


00:54:54.851 --> 00:54:57.726
to get used to the fact that
I wasn't here anymore,


00:54:57.810 --> 00:55:00.226
but they never said anything
to stop me.


00:55:00.309 --> 00:55:02.768
They never said,
"No, stay. We want you here."


00:55:02.851 --> 00:55:04.978
They just said, "Fine.
If you wanna leave, leave."


00:55:07.478 --> 00:55:11.394
I've always kinda just wondered
what was wrong with me.


00:55:11.478 --> 00:55:14.269
Why did all these families
just give up on me?


00:55:14.353 --> 00:55:16.853
Why didn't any other--


00:55:16.937 --> 00:55:19.896
any of my other family members
take me in


00:55:19.979 --> 00:55:22.354
instead of just watching me
get passed around?


00:55:23.813 --> 00:55:26.479
Definitely,
one of the biggest reasons


00:55:26.562 --> 00:55:28.480
why I'm trying so hard
not to give up


00:55:28.563 --> 00:55:31.397
and to keep pushing forward
is to show my mom.


00:55:31.481 --> 00:55:33.065
Look who you called a mistake.


00:55:33.147 --> 00:55:35.231
Look who you tried killing
several times.


00:55:35.314 --> 00:55:37.439
Like, look.
Look at who I-- who I can be.


00:55:37.523 --> 00:55:38.940
Look at where I'm going.


00:55:39.023 --> 00:55:43.106
And I just kinda wanna show her,
like, I wasn't a mistake.


00:55:53.233 --> 00:55:57.858
Being in a group home
is like lockdown.


00:55:57.942 --> 00:55:59.943
It's like having
a big ol' chain


00:56:00.026 --> 00:56:01.317
attached to your leg.


00:56:02.943 --> 00:56:05.484
You go,
and if it don't work out,


00:56:05.567 --> 00:56:08.359
you go somewhere
completely different.


00:56:10.526 --> 00:56:13.652
It's like you're just bouncing


00:56:13.736 --> 00:56:16.945
from place, to place, to place,
to place, to place.


00:56:17.028 --> 00:56:18.611
You don't know
none of these people.


00:56:18.694 --> 00:56:20.653
These people is brand new.
This is a whole new experience.


00:56:20.736 --> 00:56:22.194
This is a whole new way.
You feel me?


00:56:22.277 --> 00:56:25.112
It's a whole new environment.
Everything is different.


00:56:29.779 --> 00:56:33.403
You give a man feet,
he gonna walk.


00:56:33.487 --> 00:56:36.278
You give a man a scooter,
he gonna scoot.


00:56:36.362 --> 00:56:39.572
You give a man a skateboard,
he gonna skate.


00:56:39.656 --> 00:56:42.114
Give me a bike,
I'ma ride that damn bike.


00:56:42.197 --> 00:56:44.073
You feel me? It's just...


00:56:48.405 --> 00:56:51.572
Damn. It's a getaway.


00:57:03.950 --> 00:57:06.076
All right, I got, um, Dasani.


00:57:06.158 --> 00:57:07.867
He's here for a JDRV,


00:57:07.951 --> 00:57:10.742
which is a six-month
progress report.


00:57:10.826 --> 00:57:13.992
It covers the services that we--
that we provided for him.


00:57:14.076 --> 00:57:16.325
My concern is what's happening
on page two.


00:57:16.408 --> 00:57:18.534
-Yes.
-My concern was behavior issue,


00:57:18.617 --> 00:57:20.535
suspended. Um,
then he started blaming


00:57:20.618 --> 00:57:23.994
the house manager and threw
a pitcher of juice at him.


00:57:25.119 --> 00:57:27.994
Not working at, um, therapy.


00:57:28.078 --> 00:57:30.828
Not working at substance abuse
because they think he is, um,


00:57:30.911 --> 00:57:34.411
supplying marijuana there,
which seriously annoys me.


00:57:34.495 --> 00:57:36.411
They're allegations that
other young people


00:57:36.495 --> 00:57:39.245
were saying that he was selling.
Those are unsubstantiated.


00:57:39.328 --> 00:57:41.495
We did not take the time
to investigate that further


00:57:41.578 --> 00:57:43.161
because he was all--


00:57:43.245 --> 00:57:44.995
If the other kids are saying it,
the likelihood is, it's true.


00:57:45.079 --> 00:57:46.830
Your Honor, there--
no one found any weed on him


00:57:46.914 --> 00:57:48.330
-or any drugs.
-And not a surprise either.


00:57:48.413 --> 00:57:49.831
-No one saw him.
-Importantly--


00:57:49.914 --> 00:57:51.081
You're saying that they're
picking on him?


00:57:51.163 --> 00:57:52.580
They're focusing on him?


00:57:52.664 --> 00:57:54.372
I'm saying that
it's com-- it's not evidence.


00:57:54.455 --> 00:57:56.247
It's not-- there's-- I mean,
you can assume it's true,


00:57:56.330 --> 00:57:57.789
-but it's-- you can assume--
-I am.


00:57:57.872 --> 00:57:59.288
-Well, that's not fair.
-Importantly,


00:57:59.372 --> 00:58:01.206
he is enrolled
in a program currently.


00:58:01.289 --> 00:58:02.915
It is school based.
It is weekly.


00:58:02.998 --> 00:58:04.206
He is attending,
and it seems to be--


00:58:04.289 --> 00:58:05.581
How often is he tested?
Do you know?


00:58:05.665 --> 00:58:07.040
I don't believe
he's tested positive since--


00:58:07.123 --> 00:58:08.581
Do we know
how often he's tested?


00:58:08.665 --> 00:58:10.123
-He has a negative.
-Yeah,


00:58:10.207 --> 00:58:11.916
he tested earlier this month,
and he tested negative.


00:58:11.999 --> 00:58:14.208
So, I've still got
these other issues going on.


00:58:14.291 --> 00:58:16.750
Um, they're working with him
in counseling.


00:58:16.834 --> 00:58:18.750
If I just read
between the lines here,


00:58:18.834 --> 00:58:21.000
he's just not engaged
in the counseling.


00:58:21.084 --> 00:58:22.583
Dasani goes to counseling.


00:58:22.667 --> 00:58:26.500
He's not refusing to go.
He is not emotionally able


00:58:26.583 --> 00:58:30.459
to talk about what he witnessed
when he was four years old.


00:58:30.543 --> 00:58:32.751
Um, I know that
last time we were here,


00:58:32.835 --> 00:58:35.168
Miss Soung had talked about
getting him involved


00:58:35.252 --> 00:58:38.044
in trauma informed therapy,
and he was terrified of that.


00:58:39.002 --> 00:58:40.628
He is now receiving his therapy


00:58:40.711 --> 00:58:42.128
at Didi Hirsch.
In the spirit of the way


00:58:42.211 --> 00:58:43.878
we discuss things
to see how they're doing,


00:58:43.962 --> 00:58:46.128
why don't we give him a chance
at Didi Hirsch?


00:58:46.211 --> 00:58:49.378
All right. Um,
so I guess I'll yell a bit...


00:58:50.878 --> 00:58:53.419
and bluster,
and he'll walk out the door


00:58:53.503 --> 00:58:56.088
unless he annoys me too much,
but he'll walk out the door.


00:58:56.170 --> 00:58:59.005
Anything else I--
anyone thinks I should do?


00:58:59.089 --> 00:59:00.797
All right. Anything else?


00:59:00.880 --> 00:59:02.505
-No.
-No? All right.


00:59:10.048 --> 00:59:12.006
All right.
So, I got a report,


00:59:12.090 --> 00:59:14.339
and I want you to know
that when I first read this,


00:59:14.422 --> 00:59:16.840
I was lookin' to detain you
for the weekend.


00:59:16.923 --> 00:59:19.172
I was not happy with what
I'm reading here about--


00:59:19.256 --> 00:59:21.174
Now, they say you threw
a pitcher of juice.


00:59:21.258 --> 00:59:23.341
Was it a pitcher
or just the water or juice?


00:59:23.424 --> 00:59:25.216
I-- I threw everything,


00:59:25.299 --> 00:59:28.758
but the only thing that really
projected was the-- the juice.


00:59:28.842 --> 00:59:31.050
You got lucky, you mean.
You missed.


00:59:31.133 --> 00:59:32.508
It's unacceptable.


00:59:32.591 --> 00:59:35.467
Something like that happens,
I'm detaining you.


00:59:35.551 --> 00:59:37.342
With regard to drug counseling,


00:59:37.425 --> 00:59:39.384
they kicked you out of
the drug counseling program


00:59:39.467 --> 00:59:40.759
'cause they think
you're selling.


00:59:41.592 --> 00:59:42.843
I've been clean, sir.


00:59:42.926 --> 00:59:45.301
I appreciate that,
but there's a lotta smoke.


00:59:45.385 --> 00:59:46.677
I'm not gonna be happy


00:59:46.760 --> 00:59:48.219
if I find out
that you're selling poison


00:59:48.302 --> 00:59:50.011
or giving poison away
to any other kids.


00:59:50.095 --> 00:59:52.136
-Yes, sir.
-Don't.


00:59:52.219 --> 00:59:54.386
And then I also am reading, um,


00:59:54.469 --> 00:59:57.719
that you were a little reluctant
with your counseling.


00:59:57.803 --> 00:59:59.219
You go to the counseling,


00:59:59.302 --> 01:00:01.887
but I'm hearing that you're not
fully engaged.


01:00:01.971 --> 01:00:04.345
-To which one, sir?
-Well, your-- your counseling.


01:00:04.428 --> 01:00:06.220
-At the group home.
-The group home?


01:00:06.303 --> 01:00:07.721
-Yes.
-I went every day


01:00:07.805 --> 01:00:09.097
-and participated.
-I know. You're going.


01:00:09.179 --> 01:00:10.555
And participated actively.


01:00:10.638 --> 01:00:12.221
You think you're
participating fully? Okay.


01:00:12.304 --> 01:00:14.721
All right. So, I'm-- I'm gonna
ask you just to pause


01:00:14.805 --> 01:00:16.347
and to think about how
you could, uh,


01:00:16.430 --> 01:00:17.931
you could participate more,


01:00:18.014 --> 01:00:19.597
how you could be engaged more
to deal with the trauma--


01:00:19.681 --> 01:00:21.222
and I-- I don't mean
to be using that word--


01:00:21.305 --> 01:00:24.264
but that you experienced in your
past to work through this.


01:00:24.347 --> 01:00:25.889
I know that it's reluctance
to talk about it.


01:00:25.973 --> 01:00:28.347
We guys don't like talking
about this stuff either,


01:00:28.430 --> 01:00:30.516
but I need you to truly try.


01:00:31.683 --> 01:00:33.516
-Yes?
-Yes, sir.


01:00:33.599 --> 01:00:35.766
So, I understand he's
working with your arts program.


01:00:35.850 --> 01:00:39.391
He's very engaged. He's a good
leader in the program.


01:00:39.474 --> 01:00:41.724
Um, he does our
music program twice a week.


01:00:41.808 --> 01:00:43.350
He's very involved with that.


01:00:43.433 --> 01:00:46.267
Like I said, he often spends
lunches there as well.


01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:48.059
Uh, and I think it's
a good outlet for him.


01:00:48.142 --> 01:00:49.600
He does express himself
in there.


01:00:49.684 --> 01:00:50.976
Good. I like it. And--


01:00:51.059 --> 01:00:52.600
Yeah,
he's been a very good addition.


01:00:52.684 --> 01:00:53.935
The thing that I--
that I take from all that


01:00:54.018 --> 01:00:55.560
is intelligence,
and I've seen that.


01:00:55.643 --> 01:00:57.519
We all see it.
Let's not waste it.


01:00:57.602 --> 01:00:59.769
Nothing worse in this world
than wasted potential.


01:00:59.853 --> 01:01:02.269
All right, Dasani, uh, um,
came--


01:01:02.352 --> 01:01:04.435
started with being
pretty upset with you.


01:01:04.519 --> 01:01:05.811
I've come around a bit here,


01:01:05.894 --> 01:01:07.811
but know I'll be
watching closely.


01:01:08.894 --> 01:01:10.020
Have a good day.


01:01:14.562 --> 01:01:15.854
The Los Angeles population


01:01:15.937 --> 01:01:18.229
is ten percent
African American.


01:01:18.312 --> 01:01:21.771
The foster care system
is 25 percent African American.


01:01:21.855 --> 01:01:24.397
That's--
it's extremely disproportionate.


01:01:25.897 --> 01:01:28.897
The foster care system
is a punitive system.


01:01:28.981 --> 01:01:31.480
Punishment is just more hurt.
Like, there is that--


01:01:31.564 --> 01:01:33.355
the saying that
hurt people hurt people.


01:01:33.438 --> 01:01:35.230
People who are hurting
hurt people.


01:01:35.313 --> 01:01:37.857
Well,
punishment is just more hurt.


01:01:46.482 --> 01:01:48.149
Everyone open your little bags.


01:01:49.274 --> 01:01:51.900
Hold it. Hold it like this.


01:01:55.525 --> 01:01:56.775
Don't spill it, please.


01:01:58.525 --> 01:02:00.567
-Ice cream.
-I don't really need it.


01:02:00.650 --> 01:02:02.608
I scream.
We all scream for ice cream.


01:02:02.692 --> 01:02:04.152
Hold it. Be careful.


01:02:09.569 --> 01:02:10.735
Denyshia was--


01:02:10.819 --> 01:02:14.277
she came to me
with an attitude.


01:02:15.610 --> 01:02:19.862
She came to me with
a lot of baggage


01:02:19.945 --> 01:02:23.153
and a lot of problems,
and the social worker told me


01:02:23.236 --> 01:02:26.028
I was gonna have some problem
with Denyshia.


01:02:26.112 --> 01:02:27.654
From day one, she said,


01:02:27.737 --> 01:02:29.988
"My father said
I had the devil in me,


01:02:30.071 --> 01:02:31.697
and he's not comin' out."


01:02:31.780 --> 01:02:34.488
She had got in her room,
under her bed, and she said,


01:02:34.572 --> 01:02:37.280
"I'm not coming out. The devil
told me not to come out."


01:02:37.363 --> 01:02:40.238
I said, "Denyshia, you don't
have no devil in you."


01:02:40.321 --> 01:02:43.030
"Yes, I do. My dad said
I have the devil in you."


01:02:43.114 --> 01:02:44.364
I said, "You know what?


01:02:44.447 --> 01:02:46.322
If he's in you,
tonight he's coming out,


01:02:46.406 --> 01:02:48.073
'cause the devil can't stay
in my house."


01:02:48.156 --> 01:02:49.698
So, she came from under the bed,
and she said,


01:02:49.781 --> 01:02:51.323
"How you gonna do it?"
I said, "I'm gonna get--


01:02:51.407 --> 01:02:53.157
watch me." I said,
"One of you kids,


01:02:53.240 --> 01:02:54.490
go open the front door.


01:02:54.574 --> 01:02:57.282
Go get me the broom.
Open the front door."


01:02:57.365 --> 01:02:59.324
I said, "Devil,
you gotta come outta here.


01:02:59.408 --> 01:03:01.783
This kid-- come outta her heart,
right now,


01:03:01.867 --> 01:03:03.199
and you get outta my house."


01:03:03.283 --> 01:03:05.491
So, I got the broom,
and I swept him out,


01:03:06.658 --> 01:03:09.117
and I told her, I said,
"Now he's gone.


01:03:09.199 --> 01:03:12.492
He's outta your heart,
and he's outta my house."


01:03:12.576 --> 01:03:15.451
And she said, "Mom, you"--
I said, "He's gone.


01:03:15.535 --> 01:03:17.451
He's gone.
Do you feel him in your heart?"


01:03:17.535 --> 01:03:19.326
"No."
I said, "He's gone.


01:03:19.410 --> 01:03:21.201
Don't ever let him
come back again."


01:03:22.869 --> 01:03:26.036
And she believed that story.
That's an old wives' story...


01:03:26.120 --> 01:03:28.036
...but I believed it, too.


01:03:30.911 --> 01:03:32.661
My hands are turning red.


01:03:33.744 --> 01:03:37.079
Okay, what will happen
if the ice melt,


01:03:37.162 --> 01:03:39.705
and it's still not done?
You put more ice in it?


01:03:39.788 --> 01:03:41.329
Well, then that means
y'all did something wrong.


01:03:41.413 --> 01:03:42.830
Y'all are not shaking
good enough.


01:03:44.204 --> 01:03:45.663
If they like milkshake, I mean,


01:03:45.746 --> 01:03:47.080
why can't you guys do that?


01:03:47.163 --> 01:03:48.371
You guys can do
whatever you want.


01:03:48.454 --> 01:03:49.746
I'm gonna make ice cream.


01:03:49.830 --> 01:03:51.998
Even if I have go
to China to get ice,


01:03:52.081 --> 01:03:53.164
I will do it.


01:03:57.247 --> 01:03:58.831
The kids need
a lot of love,


01:03:58.914 --> 01:04:01.665
and the type of love,
it has to be long suffering.


01:04:01.748 --> 01:04:04.206
It have to be beyond measure.


01:04:04.290 --> 01:04:06.791
You know, you just can't
just say, "I love you."


01:04:06.875 --> 01:04:07.958
Prove it.


01:04:09.083 --> 01:04:11.833
Do something about it.
Show them.


01:04:11.916 --> 01:04:13.374
There, it's coming out.


01:04:13.457 --> 01:04:15.708
It's not coming out.
It would've been done already.


01:04:17.000 --> 01:04:18.624
I'm gonna go in the house.


01:04:18.708 --> 01:04:20.292
Okay,
let's wait until she come back.


01:04:20.375 --> 01:04:22.876
Set that-- put that down, Jake.
Let them do it.


01:04:22.959 --> 01:04:25.043
When she come back out here,
just tell her, "Okay, Denyshia,


01:04:25.127 --> 01:04:26.918
you done a good job.
We've been waiting on you."


01:04:27.002 --> 01:04:30.501
Blah, blah, blah. Give her some
encouragement, okay? Sydney?


01:04:30.585 --> 01:04:31.751
-Yes?
-Can you do that?


01:04:31.835 --> 01:04:33.377
-Yes.
-Okay, when she come back out,


01:04:33.460 --> 01:04:35.003
-just pump her up.
-That's fine with me.


01:04:35.086 --> 01:04:37.335
Okay. But don't overdo it, okay?


01:04:37.419 --> 01:04:39.003
-Teamwork.
-Teamwork, okay.


01:04:39.086 --> 01:04:41.794
You're doing an amazing job,
and something else.


01:04:41.878 --> 01:04:43.711
Okay,
you think of that.


01:04:43.794 --> 01:04:45.335
Teamwork make perfect.


01:04:45.419 --> 01:04:47.921
Teamwork--
Teamwork make perfect.


01:04:48.005 --> 01:04:49.130
That's right.


01:04:49.212 --> 01:04:50.838
Okay,
let me go get her outta there.


01:04:50.921 --> 01:04:52.337
Everybody think of something,


01:04:52.421 --> 01:04:55.629
but don't overdo it.
Okay, something to say.


01:04:55.713 --> 01:04:58.171
No, she's too irritated.


01:05:01.547 --> 01:05:02.589
I got a new idea.


01:05:02.672 --> 01:05:04.714
-Okay.
-Yippee.


01:05:04.797 --> 01:05:06.964
I'm gonna pour this ice water
over you.


01:05:07.047 --> 01:05:08.131
Go ahead.


01:05:09.255 --> 01:05:10.798
Go ahead. I don't care.


01:05:15.674 --> 01:05:19.133
I love this family. It's like
the only family I have.


01:05:19.215 --> 01:05:21.549
It's like... I really love them.


01:05:21.632 --> 01:05:23.674
Like, I don't know
what I'd be without them.


01:05:52.343 --> 01:05:53.887
-Yes?
-Hi, I'm Christina.


01:05:53.970 --> 01:05:55.137
I'm the person
you've been talking to.


01:05:55.219 --> 01:05:56.721
-Mm-hmm.
-This is my supervisor,


01:05:56.804 --> 01:05:58.304
Robin Kovalsky.
So she's just here with us.


01:05:58.387 --> 01:05:59.387
Okay.


01:06:06.888 --> 01:06:09.555
Oh, look at how cute she is.
She's adorable.


01:06:09.638 --> 01:06:10.847
-Thank you.
-Is she eating?


01:06:10.930 --> 01:06:12.430
Yeah.


01:06:12.513 --> 01:06:14.346
-Aww, great.


01:06:14.430 --> 01:06:15.680
How old is she now?


01:06:15.763 --> 01:06:18.722
-She is a month and nine days.
-Oh, okay.


01:06:18.805 --> 01:06:21.141
-She's doing really good though.
-Yeah, she is.


01:06:21.223 --> 01:06:22.765
-I don't see any concerns.
-Uh-uh.


01:06:22.849 --> 01:06:24.557
Let's go get your bassinet.


01:06:30.891 --> 01:06:32.932
Does she like going out
on walks in her stroller?


01:06:33.016 --> 01:06:35.808
Yeah. When I walk to the store,
by the time...


01:06:35.892 --> 01:06:37.308
by the time
I get to the front gate,


01:06:37.391 --> 01:06:38.850
-she's already asleep.


01:06:38.933 --> 01:06:40.391
Yeah, that's--
I know.


01:06:40.474 --> 01:06:41.975
-That's-- it's so soothing.
-She's already asleep.


01:06:42.058 --> 01:06:43.767
It's wonderful.
Yeah, it's great.


01:06:43.851 --> 01:06:45.601
Yeah, it is.


01:06:46.601 --> 01:06:47.601
Yeah.


01:06:47.684 --> 01:06:49.226
I know she's little,


01:06:49.310 --> 01:06:52.393
but what kind of baby
would you describe her as?


01:06:52.476 --> 01:06:54.185
-A happy baby.
-A happy baby.


01:06:54.268 --> 01:06:55.393
A happy baby.


01:07:10.604 --> 01:07:11.812
You don't want nothing
right now?


01:07:11.896 --> 01:07:13.187
She's kinda frozen, huh?


01:07:13.270 --> 01:07:14.312
Yeah, she is.


01:07:14.395 --> 01:07:15.688
Hey. Hey, sweetness.


01:07:15.771 --> 01:07:17.521
-She's kind of frozen.
-She's kind of frozen.


01:07:17.605 --> 01:07:19.813
-Hey, baby girl.
-Oh, she's daydreaming.


01:07:22.479 --> 01:07:23.771
-Hey.
-Does she do that often?


01:07:23.855 --> 01:07:25.771
She daydreams constantly.


01:07:27.730 --> 01:07:29.481
-I don't know.
-Hmm.


01:07:29.565 --> 01:07:31.690
When you take her back
for her shots,


01:07:31.773 --> 01:07:33.065
tell the doctor about that.


01:07:33.149 --> 01:07:34.523
Yeah, her daydreaming?


01:07:34.607 --> 01:07:36.481
-Yeah.
-Yeah, um, 'cause she, like,


01:07:36.565 --> 01:07:37.732
she was really kind of frozen.


01:07:37.815 --> 01:07:39.815
That might be
a little concerning.


01:07:39.899 --> 01:07:42.316
Yeah. Um,
sometimes it could be nothing,


01:07:42.399 --> 01:07:44.232
but sometimes it could be
a sign of something


01:07:44.316 --> 01:07:45.774
-going on in the brain.
-Okay.


01:07:45.858 --> 01:07:48.316
So we wanna make sure
the doctor is aware of that.


01:07:48.399 --> 01:07:49.691
Yeah.


01:07:49.774 --> 01:07:51.191
Yeah,
'cause she didn't even blink...


01:07:51.274 --> 01:07:52.441
No, she didn't blink at all.


01:07:52.524 --> 01:07:54.317
...for a good amount of time.


01:07:54.400 --> 01:07:55.400
-Yeah, yeah.
-Okay.


01:07:55.483 --> 01:07:56.610
And she had no movement.


01:07:56.693 --> 01:07:59.651
It was like
she was completely...


01:07:59.735 --> 01:08:03.318
Yes. Yeah, let's make sure she--
you get that followed up on.


01:08:04.693 --> 01:08:06.027
When's the doctor's appointment?


01:08:06.110 --> 01:08:07.359
The sixth.


01:08:07.443 --> 01:08:08.943
Do you know what?
Maybe you should call


01:08:09.027 --> 01:08:10.652
and see if you can get her in
before that.


01:08:10.736 --> 01:08:11.736
Okay.


01:08:11.819 --> 01:08:13.527
And let them just check her out.


01:08:14.527 --> 01:08:15.569
Okay.


01:08:15.652 --> 01:08:17.236
I mean,
everything else looks great,


01:08:17.320 --> 01:08:20.528
but that was
a little bit of a concern.


01:08:38.031 --> 01:08:39.865
Uh, let me hold her again,
and...


01:08:39.948 --> 01:08:43.573
I'm-- I'm-- uh,
so, her head control is good.


01:08:46.573 --> 01:08:48.364
Uh, don't worry.
I won't drop her.


01:08:48.448 --> 01:08:50.490
I'm just checking how she
controls


01:08:50.574 --> 01:08:52.532
-not just her arms and legs...


01:08:52.616 --> 01:08:54.490
...but her trunk and head.


01:08:54.574 --> 01:08:56.866
You know,
what people are concerned about,


01:08:56.949 --> 01:08:58.199
sounds like, is, you know,


01:08:58.282 --> 01:09:01.282
did your daughter have
a little seizure?


01:09:01.365 --> 01:09:04.659
Your brain is obviously
composed of a bunch of neurons,


01:09:04.743 --> 01:09:08.701
brain cells, and they all have,
uh, electronic properties,


01:09:08.784 --> 01:09:10.201
like telephone wires.


01:09:10.284 --> 01:09:13.367
A seizure is uncontrolled
electrical activity.


01:09:13.451 --> 01:09:18.368
You know, she's had one episode,
which is a little concerning.


01:09:18.452 --> 01:09:20.493
We-- I'd like to do a test.


01:09:20.577 --> 01:09:24.119
Uh, I have to order it. It's
called an electroencephalogram.


01:09:26.620 --> 01:09:31.537
We put about ten pairs of wires
on-- on her scalp,


01:09:31.621 --> 01:09:34.329
and we try to see if her brain


01:09:34.412 --> 01:09:37.537
has any abnormal
electrical signal,


01:09:37.621 --> 01:09:41.704
which could be a sign that she's
at risk for seizures.


01:09:41.787 --> 01:09:43.746
-Oh, okay, okay, okay,


01:09:43.829 --> 01:09:47.455
okay, okay, okay. Mama's sorry.


01:09:52.789 --> 01:09:54.414
I know.


01:10:38.087 --> 01:10:39.879
It's been about ten years


01:10:39.962 --> 01:10:42.754
since I'm at
Nurse-Family Partnership.


01:10:42.837 --> 01:10:44.670
I talk about you guys
all the time.


01:10:46.129 --> 01:10:47.420
I met Doljai, my nurse,


01:10:47.503 --> 01:10:49.712
when I was about four
or five months pregnant


01:10:49.795 --> 01:10:51.378
with my first son, Noah.


01:10:51.462 --> 01:10:55.838
After I decided to keep Noah,
I had a lotta ideas and dreams


01:10:55.922 --> 01:10:58.713
of how I would disrupt
the cycles of violence


01:10:58.796 --> 01:11:00.297
and poverty in my life,


01:11:00.380 --> 01:11:03.547
and prevent Noah
from experiencing my fate.


01:11:03.631 --> 01:11:06.215
These ideas included
unconditional love,


01:11:06.298 --> 01:11:07.882
never throwing him away,


01:11:07.965 --> 01:11:10.049
and providing him
with all the material things


01:11:10.132 --> 01:11:11.798
I longed for as a child.


01:11:11.882 --> 01:11:12.882
Doljai.


01:11:14.174 --> 01:11:15.840
-Aww.


01:11:23.884 --> 01:11:25.634
I--
Doljai Chaikumnerd--


01:11:25.717 --> 01:11:27.176
I would never remember
a name like that.


01:11:27.258 --> 01:11:28.634
I'm from South Central L.A.


01:11:32.801 --> 01:11:35.426
We don't have names like that
in South Central L.A.


01:11:38.343 --> 01:11:42.343
I had-- I had no idea
that it would take so much more


01:11:42.426 --> 01:11:44.218
than love and good intentions.


01:11:46.054 --> 01:11:47.428
Don't be surprising me.


01:11:50.678 --> 01:11:52.511
When I first met my nurse,
Doljai,


01:11:52.595 --> 01:11:54.511
I didn't think
she was gonna work out.


01:11:56.803 --> 01:11:59.387
One of her first comments
was that I shouldn't be drinking


01:11:59.471 --> 01:12:04.180
two-liter sodas and eating
Top Ramen noodles every day.


01:12:04.262 --> 01:12:06.763
But, after she told me
about the lack of nutrition


01:12:06.846 --> 01:12:08.096
in my diet


01:12:08.180 --> 01:12:10.181
and how my feet were gonna
swell up like sausages,


01:12:10.263 --> 01:12:11.931
I threw it all away.


01:12:12.014 --> 01:12:13.556
Doljai was like a human


01:12:13.640 --> 01:12:16.681
"What to Expect When You're
Expecting" app on steroids.


01:12:19.556 --> 01:12:21.015
The only other voices in my life


01:12:21.098 --> 01:12:23.182
were my child's
father's parents.


01:12:23.264 --> 01:12:25.514
"Don't hold that baby,
or you're gonna spoil him.


01:12:25.598 --> 01:12:27.432
Don't do this,
or you're gonna do that."


01:12:27.515 --> 01:12:29.058
I was able to say,
"Well, my nurse,


01:12:29.141 --> 01:12:30.515
she has a degree,
and she said this,


01:12:30.599 --> 01:12:32.391
and here is a handout
if you need to read it."


01:12:32.475 --> 01:12:35.225
-So--


01:12:35.308 --> 01:12:38.225
So it was very
empowering for me.


01:12:38.308 --> 01:12:42.684
Today, Noah is nine, and his
brother, Jonah, is seven.


01:12:42.768 --> 01:12:44.392
They play basketball
and football


01:12:44.476 --> 01:12:45.809
and get good grades.


01:12:48.018 --> 01:12:50.101
Noah is
a self-proclaimed vegetarian,


01:12:51.476 --> 01:12:53.309
and Jonah eats
everything in sight.


01:12:55.477 --> 01:12:56.853
They have never known
what it's like


01:12:56.937 --> 01:12:59.020
to be abused or neglected


01:12:59.103 --> 01:13:01.353
or had to wonder
if they are loved,


01:13:01.436 --> 01:13:02.561
and they never will.


01:13:04.394 --> 01:13:07.312
I have been very privileged
to have had many people


01:13:07.395 --> 01:13:09.854
positively impact my life.


01:13:09.938 --> 01:13:12.687
However, my experience working
with youth and families


01:13:12.771 --> 01:13:16.104
as they overcome
tremendous obstacles to success


01:13:16.188 --> 01:13:18.687
has taught me that sometimes
it just takes one.


01:13:19.521 --> 01:13:21.231
I needed you.


01:13:21.314 --> 01:13:24.522
I also know that, at 18,
I didn't love myself,


01:13:24.606 --> 01:13:26.272
and I didn't care about myself,


01:13:26.356 --> 01:13:29.731
so I needed someone to care
what happened to my child


01:13:29.814 --> 01:13:32.023
as I was becoming who I am now.


01:13:34.690 --> 01:13:36.899
So, I just wanted
to thank you guys so much


01:13:36.982 --> 01:13:38.273
for all you do,


01:13:38.357 --> 01:13:40.941
and, hopefully, inspire you guys
so just one of 'em


01:13:41.024 --> 01:13:44.025
-out of your 50,000...


01:13:44.108 --> 01:13:46.566
...hopefully, one of them makes
you very proud.


01:13:59.776 --> 01:14:01.235
You did a good job.


01:14:05.861 --> 01:14:07.778
You tricked me into doing this.


01:14:12.569 --> 01:14:14.361
Order 44C is now in session.


01:14:14.444 --> 01:14:16.820
The honorable Margaret S. Henry,
Judge, presiding.


01:14:22.445 --> 01:14:25.278
Hey, guys. Okay?


01:14:26.112 --> 01:14:27.695
Good morning.


01:14:27.779 --> 01:14:30.614
Hello. My stomach hurts.


01:14:30.697 --> 01:14:34.447
All right. Counsel,
state your appearances, please.


01:14:34.530 --> 01:14:37.948
Jennifer Lorson, CLCLA 4
on behalf of the youth.


01:14:38.031 --> 01:14:39.405
She goes by Mary, Your Honor.


01:14:39.489 --> 01:14:41.405
Mary is present and seated
at counsel table.


01:14:41.489 --> 01:14:43.531
Mary,
what can we do for you today?


01:14:43.615 --> 01:14:45.698
Let's see. One of the things
that didn't happen is,


01:14:45.782 --> 01:14:47.573
she did not get
immediately linked to


01:14:47.657 --> 01:14:50.990
the Resilient Scholars over at
Cal State University Northridge


01:14:51.074 --> 01:14:53.241
to try to get her--
Oh, well, then that's true.


01:14:53.324 --> 01:14:54.950
I also heard
that she's thinking about


01:14:55.033 --> 01:14:56.825
not wanting to continue
in school.


01:14:56.909 --> 01:14:59.242
No. What? Stop. Oh, my gosh.


01:14:59.325 --> 01:15:01.700
Oh, you're thinking about
dropping out. Oh.


01:15:01.784 --> 01:15:04.867
No, no, not-- Um...


01:15:04.951 --> 01:15:07.034
Just out of that school.


01:15:07.117 --> 01:15:10.576
I want to go to
an all-performing-arts academy.


01:15:10.660 --> 01:15:12.243
What's your career goal?


01:15:12.326 --> 01:15:14.035
I want to be an actress.


01:15:14.118 --> 01:15:16.994
That's a tough one.
You know, it's very difficult.


01:15:17.494 --> 01:15:18.577
I know.


01:15:18.661 --> 01:15:21.661
Um, yeah,
and a lot of competition.


01:15:21.744 --> 01:15:24.495
You graduated from high school.
That's a big deal,


01:15:24.578 --> 01:15:26.703
but I think even as--
as an actress,


01:15:26.787 --> 01:15:30.245
if you could study acting,
um, in college,


01:15:30.328 --> 01:15:32.162
it'll help you
with everything in life.


01:15:32.245 --> 01:15:34.162
-Okay.
-You know, if you--


01:15:34.245 --> 01:15:36.329
if you get some more education.


01:15:37.537 --> 01:15:38.872
So, this semester was definitely


01:15:38.956 --> 01:15:41.622
really, really hard for me,
and, uh...


01:15:41.705 --> 01:15:45.206
I saw a therapist, and,
you know, they told me,


01:15:45.288 --> 01:15:48.830
"It'd be best to take a medical
leave for you, right now."


01:15:48.914 --> 01:15:50.498
It doesn't mean
I'm not going back.


01:15:50.581 --> 01:15:51.957
It doesn't mean I'm, like,
you know,


01:15:52.040 --> 01:15:53.373
completely through with it.


01:15:53.456 --> 01:15:55.165
It just means, right now,
I am not ready.


01:16:05.416 --> 01:16:08.458
I'm not really interested in
people that want to be famous.


01:16:08.541 --> 01:16:09.792
-Mm-hmm.
-I'm interested in people


01:16:09.875 --> 01:16:11.291
that really want to be artists,


01:16:11.375 --> 01:16:13.708
because so few people
become famous,


01:16:13.792 --> 01:16:15.000
and I don't think it's really


01:16:15.084 --> 01:16:16.416
all it's cracked up
to be anyway.


01:16:17.334 --> 01:16:18.960
What calls you to acting?


01:16:19.043 --> 01:16:22.210
In theater, when I was on stage,
when I'd perform,


01:16:23.542 --> 01:16:26.877
I'd forget about everything.


01:16:28.835 --> 01:16:30.503
It made me happy.


01:16:30.586 --> 01:16:36.003
It felt nice to pretend to be
somebody else for a moment.


01:16:36.087 --> 01:16:40.711
I just also get to imagine
another life for me


01:16:40.795 --> 01:16:42.836
when I have a-- a--
a certain character.


01:16:42.920 --> 01:16:46.295
I can, you know, build up
her background story


01:16:46.379 --> 01:16:49.505
and pretend that it's mine, and,
you know,


01:16:49.588 --> 01:16:51.964
depending on the character,
most of the time,


01:16:52.047 --> 01:16:56.546
I give myself a happy
childhood memory and, like,


01:16:56.630 --> 01:16:59.756
family and stuff, and it's--
it's nice to...


01:16:59.839 --> 01:17:01.381
I guess, kind of imagine that.


01:17:06.923 --> 01:17:09.006
Come in and get a chair.


01:17:09.090 --> 01:17:11.799
Closer in the circle, please.


01:17:12.758 --> 01:17:14.299
The floor is cool.


01:17:16.299 --> 01:17:19.092
All right, our topic tonight
is what?


01:17:19.800 --> 01:17:20.925
Family.


01:17:21.008 --> 01:17:22.883
Man, the shit starts at home.


01:17:22.967 --> 01:17:27.968
If stuff is not good at home,
it's really difficult.


01:17:29.801 --> 01:17:33.093
We got a beautiful family
in the room right now. Um,


01:17:33.176 --> 01:17:35.093
I don't know where I'd
be without it.


01:17:35.176 --> 01:17:38.011
So, um, Dasani, are you ready,
or you want a little more time?


01:17:38.095 --> 01:17:39.761
Uh, I'm ready, right now.


01:17:41.928 --> 01:17:43.344
How y'all doin' tonight?


01:17:45.636 --> 01:17:47.636
I'm doin' real good.


01:17:47.719 --> 01:17:50.511
This is a sensitive top--
topic tonight,


01:17:50.594 --> 01:17:53.470
so it's-- Man, never mind.
Just let me get to it.


01:17:53.553 --> 01:17:56.345
Let me get down to it.
Let me get down to it. Ah.


01:17:56.428 --> 01:17:58.680
There's only one thing
I want you to work on.


01:17:58.763 --> 01:18:01.763
-Yes, ma'am.
-You and I share something else,


01:18:01.846 --> 01:18:03.763
and you do it every time
you get up,


01:18:03.846 --> 01:18:05.681
something I do, too.
What is it?


01:18:07.681 --> 01:18:08.681
Goofy?


01:18:10.681 --> 01:18:14.098
You always smile. It's easier,
right? It's a good--


01:18:14.181 --> 01:18:15.555
It's just a natural feeling


01:18:15.639 --> 01:18:18.390
when you're around what you
wanna be around,


01:18:18.473 --> 01:18:19.765
'cause you don't get
to be around


01:18:19.848 --> 01:18:21.891
what you really wanna
be around a lot,


01:18:21.975 --> 01:18:23.683
and I feel that here.
I really feel that.


01:18:23.766 --> 01:18:26.225
This is the only place.
So, I appreciate y'all.


01:18:26.307 --> 01:18:27.808
That's awesome.


01:18:27.891 --> 01:18:32.059
So, um, take a deep breath
and give us your piece.


01:18:38.725 --> 01:18:42.142
♪ Look
Some live in the gutter ♪


01:18:42.226 --> 01:18:43.850
♪ Some were born
With silver spoons ♪


01:18:43.934 --> 01:18:45.309
♪ Born in the world-- ♪
Can we turn that down?


01:18:45.393 --> 01:18:46.727
-Goddamn!
-Yeah, we gotta


01:18:46.811 --> 01:18:47.936
-turn it down and start again.


01:18:48.019 --> 01:18:49.519
Hold on. It's okay.


01:18:50.727 --> 01:18:51.978
Let's start it all over again.


01:18:52.061 --> 01:18:54.394
-Good Lord.
-We're good.


01:18:54.477 --> 01:18:56.477
All right, let me get it
all the way back,


01:18:56.560 --> 01:18:57.894
one more time, please.


01:18:57.978 --> 01:18:59.478
You wanna do it
one time without the music


01:18:59.561 --> 01:19:01.020
-and then come back with it?
-I wanna do it


01:19:01.104 --> 01:19:02.645
one time without the music
real quick. Yeah.


01:19:02.728 --> 01:19:04.478
Can we just
stop the music for a sec? So...


01:19:04.561 --> 01:19:06.770
All right, so acapella one time,
and then I'll get into the beat.


01:19:06.853 --> 01:19:08.521
Yeah,
I wanna hear. Just--


01:19:08.604 --> 01:19:10.146
♪ Some live in the gutter ♪


01:19:10.230 --> 01:19:11.479
♪ Some are born
With silver spoons ♪


01:19:11.562 --> 01:19:13.064
♪ Born into the world ♪


01:19:13.147 --> 01:19:14.647
♪ Live your life
You don't get to choose ♪


01:19:14.730 --> 01:19:16.272
♪ Grow up in society ♪


01:19:16.355 --> 01:19:18.189
♪ Deciding if you should
Break the rules ♪


01:19:18.272 --> 01:19:19.605
♪ We were brought up
In the world blind ♪


01:19:19.689 --> 01:19:21.022
♪ Without a clue ♪


01:19:21.106 --> 01:19:22.772
♪ Everybody got
The same motive ♪


01:19:22.855 --> 01:19:24.522
♪ Tryin' to find
Who's really you ♪


01:19:24.605 --> 01:19:25.939
♪ They're allegedly
My mother and father ♪


01:19:26.022 --> 01:19:27.690
♪ But is it even true? ♪


01:19:27.773 --> 01:19:29.856
♪ Whenever I think about 'em
I get sick like I got the flu ♪


01:19:29.940 --> 01:19:32.315
♪ 'Cause I had no father figure
And I ain't know what to do ♪


01:19:32.399 --> 01:19:34.274
♪ Everybody talk about
They mom and pop ♪


01:19:34.357 --> 01:19:36.357
♪ Talk about family
Or backyard cookouts ♪


01:19:36.440 --> 01:19:39.357
♪ Everybody chillin' happily
Lost mama but I pick-- ♪


01:19:39.440 --> 01:19:40.650
Ooh, hold up. Hold up.


01:19:40.733 --> 01:19:42.358
-Ooh, damn.
-Why don't you sit down.


01:19:42.441 --> 01:19:44.358
-Yeah. Jesus.
-Just have a seat.


01:19:44.441 --> 01:19:47.525
-I know. This is a lot.
-Yeah, hold up.


01:19:47.608 --> 01:19:49.234
♪ 'Cause ain't I got
No father figure ♪


01:19:49.316 --> 01:19:50.942
♪ And I ain't know what to do ♪


01:19:51.025 --> 01:19:52.858
♪ All that mom-and-pop talk
And family ♪


01:19:52.942 --> 01:19:55.318
♪ Or backyard cookouts
Everybody chillin' happily ♪


01:19:55.402 --> 01:19:56.860
♪ Lost my mama ♪


01:19:56.944 --> 01:19:58.610
♪ But all I did
Was pick up gradually ♪


01:19:58.694 --> 01:20:00.402
♪ I didn't know
till I got older ♪


01:20:00.485 --> 01:20:02.069
♪ It was my worst tragedy ♪


01:20:02.152 --> 01:20:06.027
♪ When I could finally count
ABC past the Z ♪


01:20:06.111 --> 01:20:08.736
♪ Kids runnin', schools out
Into their arms ♪


01:20:08.820 --> 01:20:10.195
♪ Passin' me ♪


01:20:10.278 --> 01:20:12.319
♪ I noticed the heartbreak
The casualty ♪


01:20:12.403 --> 01:20:14.653
♪ That experience
Changed my mentality ♪


01:20:14.736 --> 01:20:17.945
♪ I start observing, listening
And reacting tactically ♪


01:20:18.028 --> 01:20:20.113
♪ 'Cause what you love
Can disappear drastically ♪


01:20:20.196 --> 01:20:21.239
♪ 'Cause-- ♪


01:20:22.905 --> 01:20:24.738
♪ 'Cause every day
People witness fatalities ♪


01:20:24.822 --> 01:20:27.363
♪ 'Cause people can't come
To a peaceful analogy ♪


01:20:27.446 --> 01:20:29.989
♪ Lost all my family
And now I got the last DNA ♪


01:20:30.072 --> 01:20:31.155
♪ In my anatomy ♪


01:20:31.239 --> 01:20:32.822
♪ People often ask questions ♪


01:20:32.905 --> 01:20:34.572
♪ 'Cause they think
It's sad to see ♪


01:20:34.656 --> 01:20:36.240
♪ I wonder why people think
It's so fascinating ♪


01:20:36.322 --> 01:20:37.781
♪ To see
a young man challenging ♪


01:20:37.864 --> 01:20:40.073
♪ Probation big way malicing ♪


01:20:40.156 --> 01:20:42.032
Hold up.


01:20:42.116 --> 01:20:43.949
I wanna try to do it
with the music, man,


01:20:44.032 --> 01:20:45.615
because it really slap
with the music.


01:20:45.699 --> 01:20:46.991
-I'm telling you all.
-I know.


01:20:47.074 --> 01:20:49.117
I was in my room,
really in there goin',


01:20:49.200 --> 01:20:51.075
-for, like, two hours.
-I already know.


01:20:51.158 --> 01:20:53.700
Honestly, like,
without the music,


01:20:53.783 --> 01:20:55.449
I can hear
exactly what you're sayin'.


01:20:55.533 --> 01:20:58.283
You know what I mean?
Like, it ain't hidin' be--


01:20:58.366 --> 01:21:00.324
What you got to say is not
hiding behind the beat,


01:21:00.408 --> 01:21:03.159
right now, and what you got
to say is so important.


01:21:03.243 --> 01:21:07.451
That was the most vulnerable
and authentic piece of you


01:21:07.535 --> 01:21:09.493
that you shared with us,


01:21:09.576 --> 01:21:10.952
and I don't know
if you're aware,


01:21:11.035 --> 01:21:12.576
but when you show up that way,


01:21:12.660 --> 01:21:14.786
it gives everyone else your
permission to do the same.


01:21:14.869 --> 01:21:16.327
Right, exactly.


01:21:16.411 --> 01:21:19.327
Because not everyone here
may have experienced the system,


01:21:19.411 --> 01:21:23.120
but I can count maybe
at least four or five of us


01:21:23.203 --> 01:21:25.203
who've experienced abandonment,


01:21:25.286 --> 01:21:29.038
who have experienced loss,
including myself.


01:21:29.122 --> 01:21:31.788
And even thinking about
my own piece today


01:21:31.871 --> 01:21:33.705
and how I wanted to deliver it,


01:21:33.788 --> 01:21:36.997
I now feel the sense of you
within it.


01:21:37.080 --> 01:21:39.329
So thank you.
Thank you for showing up.


01:21:39.413 --> 01:21:41.413
Appreciate that, really do,


01:21:42.414 --> 01:21:43.998
all y'all, for real.


01:21:53.581 --> 01:21:57.083
He told me that he felt like
these last couple of years


01:21:57.166 --> 01:21:58.374
has been a waste of his life.


01:21:58.457 --> 01:21:59.958
-You know, he's just--
-Right.


01:22:00.041 --> 01:22:02.374
Time is just passing
until you get into adulthood.


01:22:02.457 --> 01:22:03.874
He's so insightful.


01:22:03.958 --> 01:22:07.208
So, he really wants that
connection, like, that family.


01:22:07.291 --> 01:22:09.500
And, like, how can you not want
that for him


01:22:09.583 --> 01:22:10.959
when that's what wants?


01:22:11.042 --> 01:22:12.750
I mean, you know, we-
we all got that phone call,


01:22:12.834 --> 01:22:15.710
ten o'clock at night,
when he was, you know,


01:22:15.793 --> 01:22:18.127
really upset and--
and about to AWOL,


01:22:18.210 --> 01:22:21.043
and, you know, Lanny and you
talked him down,


01:22:21.127 --> 01:22:22.711
-calmed him down, and he stayed.
-Mm-hmm.


01:22:22.794 --> 01:22:25.544
I don't know. With all this,
I mean, you know,


01:22:25.627 --> 01:22:30.544
with all the effort around it,
I still fear that one day


01:22:30.627 --> 01:22:32.919
not bein'-- not bein' able
to answer the phone, you know?


01:22:33.003 --> 01:22:34.585
-Right. Yeah.
-And him--


01:22:34.669 --> 01:22:37.087
And-- And--
my fear is, also, is, even if


01:22:37.170 --> 01:22:39.379
you hadn't answered the phone,
and he'd taken off, I mean,


01:22:39.462 --> 01:22:42.379
knowing Dasani, he could
come back later that night


01:22:42.462 --> 01:22:46.255
or the next day,
and the consequences for that...


01:22:46.337 --> 01:22:47.337
Yeah.


01:22:47.421 --> 01:22:48.963
...are so much more severe,


01:22:49.046 --> 01:22:52.672
again, than a non-foster youth,
or a non-probation youth.


01:22:52.755 --> 01:22:55.463
If he'd been at home
with two brothers...


01:22:55.547 --> 01:22:57.338
-Oh, yeah.
-...and they'd got in a fight,


01:22:57.422 --> 01:22:58.714
-and somebody, you know...
Right.


01:22:58.797 --> 01:23:00.131
...got angry and threw a book,


01:23:00.214 --> 01:23:01.923
-and it broke the window...
-Right.


01:23:02.007 --> 01:23:05.216
You know, the three brothers
would have been grounded.


01:23:05.299 --> 01:23:06.632
Right.


01:23:06.716 --> 01:23:08.882
And our kid gets thrown in jail
and charged with felony.


01:23:08.966 --> 01:23:10.133
With a felony.


01:23:10.216 --> 01:23:13.049
And I see this over
and over and over,


01:23:13.966 --> 01:23:15.174
and it's just so unfair.


01:23:26.135 --> 01:23:27.843
Can we talk about
your appointment?


01:23:27.926 --> 01:23:31.343
Your neurologist appointment?
How that went?


01:23:31.427 --> 01:23:32.510
Uh...


01:23:35.969 --> 01:23:37.719
I was hurt
for the simple fact


01:23:37.802 --> 01:23:41.760
just that now my daughter might
have to deal with something


01:23:41.844 --> 01:23:44.845
that I feel she shouldn't
have to deal with


01:23:44.928 --> 01:23:48.929
for the simple fact of
what her mother done did.


01:23:49.013 --> 01:23:50.179
But, um...


01:23:52.929 --> 01:23:56.637
um, I already knew
it was a possibility,


01:23:56.721 --> 01:23:59.972
but it's just the simple fact
of just knowin'


01:24:00.055 --> 01:24:04.055
that she can have a seizure, or
just knowin' that type of stuff.


01:24:04.139 --> 01:24:07.555
You know, I already have a lot
on my-- on my plate, you know,


01:24:07.638 --> 01:24:11.515
with just trying to juggle
working and being a parent.


01:24:11.598 --> 01:24:13.057
You know, it's--


01:24:13.141 --> 01:24:16.016
-Overwhelming.
-Yeah, it's very overwhelming.


01:24:16.099 --> 01:24:18.182
Now I see what single mothers
go through.


01:24:19.432 --> 01:24:21.348
And it's hard.
It is-- it is very hard,


01:24:21.432 --> 01:24:23.807
'cause I'm not the type that
wanna be sitting on the county,


01:24:23.890 --> 01:24:25.683
collectin' money.
I'm not the type of person


01:24:25.766 --> 01:24:27.349
that wanna be sittin' on my butt


01:24:27.433 --> 01:24:30.474
'cause I know that's not what
a man is supposed to be doing.


01:24:30.558 --> 01:24:32.142
And, right now,
I feel like


01:24:32.225 --> 01:24:34.851
that's what's gonna be
having to happen for a while.


01:24:34.934 --> 01:24:36.976
With the neurologist, I was...


01:24:39.435 --> 01:24:42.185
Excuse my language,
I was pissed off


01:24:42.269 --> 01:24:43.560
for the simple fact


01:24:43.643 --> 01:24:45.185
just that Raeanne
done did something,


01:24:45.269 --> 01:24:46.852
and it's-- and it's gonna--


01:24:46.935 --> 01:24:48.977
it's gonna reflect
on my daughter.


01:24:53.103 --> 01:24:55.853
Why the hell did you choose
to do this fucking drug?


01:24:59.353 --> 01:25:01.312
Can I share something, Chris?


01:25:01.395 --> 01:25:05.022
Just hearing you talk now,


01:25:05.105 --> 01:25:08.272
the benefits of having
someone that you can talk to


01:25:08.354 --> 01:25:09.896
and express
some of those feelings


01:25:09.980 --> 01:25:12.688
and get that
off your chest because,


01:25:12.771 --> 01:25:14.938
yes, mom made a mistake.
Mom, I think,


01:25:15.022 --> 01:25:19.731
is going through her own
processes, as well as you are.


01:25:19.814 --> 01:25:21.356
You guys have to come to a--


01:25:21.440 --> 01:25:24.523
a point where you
have to forgive her.


01:25:24.606 --> 01:25:27.398
-She has to forgive herself.
-And I-- and I-- I do.


01:25:27.481 --> 01:25:28.732
-I do forgive her...
-Yeah.


01:25:28.815 --> 01:25:30.523
...and I'm still gonna be
here for her.


01:25:30.606 --> 01:25:34.275
It's just that--
that it was just like, wow,


01:25:34.357 --> 01:25:36.524
I had to leave out the room
for a second


01:25:36.607 --> 01:25:39.816
and go walk 'cause it--
it put tears in my eyes


01:25:39.899 --> 01:25:42.566
just 'cause my daughter
is gonna have to deal with that,


01:25:42.649 --> 01:25:43.983
but...


01:25:44.066 --> 01:25:45.776
And I think that
counseling piece,


01:25:45.860 --> 01:25:47.027
and not to cut you off,


01:25:47.110 --> 01:25:49.735
but that counseling piece
can assist you.


01:25:49.818 --> 01:25:50.943
I know sometimes--


01:25:51.027 --> 01:25:52.277
You're trying
to get me counseling.


01:25:52.359 --> 01:25:53.943
-I know. I'm gonna--


01:25:54.027 --> 01:25:55.401
-I'm workin' it in.
-Trying to get me counseling.


01:25:55.484 --> 01:25:56.943
And I'm not--
And it's not, like--


01:25:57.027 --> 01:25:58.944
I think sometimes being--
even just being an, um,


01:25:59.028 --> 01:26:00.610
African American young man,
you're like,


01:26:00.694 --> 01:26:02.485
"I don't need no one to talk to.
Like, I'm a man.


01:26:02.569 --> 01:26:04.111
I can take care of this myself."


01:26:04.194 --> 01:26:07.319
But you may need somebody
just to spend 30 minutes with


01:26:07.402 --> 01:26:10.279
to say, "You know what?
I'm frustrated. I'm tired.


01:26:10.361 --> 01:26:11.570
Like, I need some help."


01:26:11.653 --> 01:26:13.529
So you don't have to
keep it all in.


01:26:16.238 --> 01:26:19.696
Can I just say that
I have been doing this


01:26:19.779 --> 01:26:20.988
for a long time...


01:26:22.487 --> 01:26:29.156
...and I am really proud
of this young man right here.


01:26:29.239 --> 01:26:31.823
-Oh, yeah.
-Twenty-four years old...


01:26:31.906 --> 01:26:35.032
-Yeah.
-...takin' the responsibility


01:26:35.115 --> 01:26:38.032
-at the levels that he is.
-Yeah.


01:26:38.115 --> 01:26:40.657
It makes me proud.


01:26:40.741 --> 01:26:45.782
Thank you, and I really do
appreciate that. I really do.


01:26:52.365 --> 01:26:53.783
Parent Partners,


01:26:53.867 --> 01:26:56.409
we are parents who have had
open cases in the past,


01:26:56.492 --> 01:26:58.993
and now we work in collaboration
with the department


01:26:59.076 --> 01:27:01.160
mainly to support you
through the whole entire time.


01:27:01.243 --> 01:27:03.451
Okay. Okay, good.


01:27:03.534 --> 01:27:05.326
So, in my case, it took me


01:27:05.409 --> 01:27:08.535
ten months to get my kids back,
but it was in program


01:27:08.618 --> 01:27:09.869
that I learned that


01:27:09.952 --> 01:27:11.577
once you're an addict,
you're always an addict.


01:27:11.660 --> 01:27:14.994
Mm-hmm.
I'm gonna admit my faults,


01:27:15.077 --> 01:27:17.286
and I'm gonna admit
that I had a problem


01:27:17.368 --> 01:27:18.703
'cause if I didn't
have a problem,


01:27:18.786 --> 01:27:20.871
I wouldn't have used
while I was pregnant.


01:27:20.954 --> 01:27:23.121
If I wouldn't have got
this open case,


01:27:23.204 --> 01:27:24.829
I could have been
one of those to use


01:27:24.912 --> 01:27:28.704
breakfast, lunch, and dinner,
and that's why I'm happy that--


01:27:28.787 --> 01:27:30.370
I'm not happy
with the situation,


01:27:30.454 --> 01:27:32.704
but I'm happy
that it stopped now,


01:27:32.787 --> 01:27:34.205
and I'm only 23,


01:27:34.289 --> 01:27:36.371
and I still have
my life ahead of me,


01:27:36.455 --> 01:27:38.663
and I still have time
to grow with my daughter


01:27:38.747 --> 01:27:43.331
and to-- to be able to see her
learn how to crawl and to walk.


01:27:43.414 --> 01:27:46.123
So I heard that you have
a-- a court date coming up.


01:27:46.206 --> 01:27:48.832
Yes. I don't really know
what to expect at court.


01:27:48.915 --> 01:27:51.415
I checked right into
outpatient treatment.


01:27:51.498 --> 01:27:53.957
I attend five days a week,
9 to 12.


01:27:54.041 --> 01:27:58.915
I attend two NA meetings, um,
outside of my treatment a week,


01:27:58.999 --> 01:28:00.457
and I'm testing clean.


01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:03.083
So I don't know
what more I can do


01:28:03.167 --> 01:28:06.834
to show that I'm taking
my consequences serious.


01:28:06.917 --> 01:28:10.251
You know, I-- I often
share with parents that, uh,


01:28:10.334 --> 01:28:12.417
-the consequences.
-I know.


01:28:12.500 --> 01:28:14.126
Not what I went through,


01:28:14.210 --> 01:28:16.752
but what I put
my children through.


01:28:16.835 --> 01:28:19.793
I'm grateful
that, you know,


01:28:19.877 --> 01:28:22.752
that my daughter is here
and she's okay


01:28:22.835 --> 01:28:25.626
because I done fucked up.


01:28:25.710 --> 01:28:28.503
That's why I don't take off
my hospital band.


01:28:29.962 --> 01:28:31.587
That's why I keep it on,
'cause it--


01:28:31.670 --> 01:28:33.837
every day, I look at it,
and I'm like,


01:28:33.920 --> 01:28:35.420
I'm doing this to get her.


01:28:37.879 --> 01:28:39.837
This is my piece of her.


01:28:59.548 --> 01:29:01.506
What a cute apartment.


01:29:01.590 --> 01:29:02.673
I told you.


01:29:02.757 --> 01:29:03.965
It's adorable.


01:29:04.049 --> 01:29:06.840
This is my side
of the apartment,


01:29:06.923 --> 01:29:08.382
and that's Silvia's.


01:29:08.466 --> 01:29:10.258
That's great.
Is this-- So, this is your room?


01:29:10.341 --> 01:29:13.091
-Yeah, but it's locked.
-Oh, right, until, yeah--


01:29:13.175 --> 01:29:14.759
But it wasn't locked before.


01:29:14.842 --> 01:29:16.925
No, because you have to
sign paperwork.


01:29:20.967 --> 01:29:23.509
-Have you opened the room?
-It's locked.


01:29:23.593 --> 01:29:25.926
-No, it's not.
-They locked it.


01:29:26.010 --> 01:29:28.551
-Why would it be locked?
-That's what I said.


01:29:31.926 --> 01:29:34.426
-Oh!
-Why don't you think, Mary?


01:29:35.802 --> 01:29:36.886
This is new.


01:29:40.304 --> 01:29:42.386
-Hey, Silvia?
-What?


01:29:42.470 --> 01:29:45.220
Are we allowed to take
the plastic off the bed?


01:29:45.304 --> 01:29:47.553
I dunno. Probably.
You just take it off.


01:29:47.636 --> 01:29:48.720
You got the papers?


01:29:48.803 --> 01:29:49.888
Yes, I do.


01:29:49.971 --> 01:29:51.346
Should we maybe
go sit down?


01:29:51.429 --> 01:29:52.804
We have a bunch of papers
we have to sign.


01:29:52.888 --> 01:29:53.929
You're gonna be
stuck over there.


01:29:54.013 --> 01:29:55.096
There we go. Okay.


01:29:55.846 --> 01:29:57.888
Okay, so.


01:29:57.971 --> 01:30:01.306
We are so happy
that you're finally, um,


01:30:01.388 --> 01:30:03.806
in this program,
and I am especially glad


01:30:03.890 --> 01:30:05.223
you get to live
with your sister.


01:30:05.307 --> 01:30:06.890
-That is just really--
-Yeah...


01:30:06.973 --> 01:30:09.389
-That is so special.


01:30:09.473 --> 01:30:11.556
I got taken away
from my sisters


01:30:11.639 --> 01:30:13.389
at, like, age seven or eight,


01:30:13.473 --> 01:30:16.599
and it wasn't till I hit 14
that I found out that


01:30:16.682 --> 01:30:18.640
Silvia went back
into the system,


01:30:18.724 --> 01:30:22.349
and they set us up for,
like, a visit.


01:30:22.432 --> 01:30:24.767
She just seemed so happy,
full of life.


01:30:24.850 --> 01:30:26.683
Like, every time
we had a visit,


01:30:26.767 --> 01:30:28.558
she'd make sure
I had so much fun.


01:30:30.351 --> 01:30:31.809
I turned 18,


01:30:31.893 --> 01:30:33.934
and I found out that she went
into transitional housing,


01:30:34.018 --> 01:30:36.060
and they said, you know,
you have the opportunity


01:30:36.143 --> 01:30:37.517
to get into this program, too,


01:30:37.601 --> 01:30:40.851
and maybe get into
the same apartment as her.


01:30:40.934 --> 01:30:43.352
I was, like, excited,
off the wall.


01:30:43.435 --> 01:30:44.935
I was-- I was-- you know,


01:30:45.019 --> 01:30:47.269
that's all I wanted
for the longest time,


01:30:47.352 --> 01:30:49.770
to be close to somebody
in my family,


01:30:49.853 --> 01:30:54.436
just 'cause it's different.
There's a different connection.


01:30:54.519 --> 01:30:57.395
Well, around 2012,
the State of California


01:30:57.479 --> 01:31:01.771
came out with a law to extend
foster care to the age of 21


01:31:01.854 --> 01:31:05.395
because they were finding
that youth were being discharged


01:31:05.479 --> 01:31:07.229
from foster care at age 18


01:31:07.313 --> 01:31:08.979
and they weren't prepared
for life.


01:31:09.063 --> 01:31:10.814
You know,
they just didn't have the funds,


01:31:10.898 --> 01:31:12.231
they didn't have
the financial basis,


01:31:12.315 --> 01:31:13.606
they didn't have the education,


01:31:13.689 --> 01:31:14.898
they didn't have
the life skills,


01:31:14.981 --> 01:31:16.939
and they didn't have
the family support


01:31:17.023 --> 01:31:18.356
to make it on their own,


01:31:18.439 --> 01:31:20.689
so there's this huge
homeless rate.


01:31:21.898 --> 01:31:24.191
But we do have expectations.


01:31:24.274 --> 01:31:28.274
You have to be either working
or going to school.


01:31:28.357 --> 01:31:30.149
We do expect you to


01:31:30.232 --> 01:31:32.857
maintain your status
with school, and, of course,


01:31:32.940 --> 01:31:34.816
we encourage you
in every capacity that way.


01:31:34.900 --> 01:31:36.983
If you need tutors,
we will get you tutors.


01:31:37.067 --> 01:31:38.151
I need tutors.


01:31:38.234 --> 01:31:41.026
-We'll do it.


01:31:42.276 --> 01:31:44.276
So, do you have any questions?


01:31:45.234 --> 01:31:46.359
Can I take off the wrap


01:31:46.442 --> 01:31:48.193
on the bedsheet?
